window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-2016"] = {"id": "jammu-2016", "title": "University of Jammu 2016", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu 2016, M.A. Psychology entrance (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "The expression that \"you can't teach an old dog new tricks\" would support which theory of forgetting?", "options": {"a": "Repression", "b": "Retroactive interference", "c": "Proactive interference", "d": "Pass interference"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the saying fits proactive interference, in which old learning gets in the way of new learning. Interference theory, championed by John McGeoch (1932), holds that we forget mainly because other memories compete with the one we want, not because traces simply fade with time. The competition can run in two directions. In proactive interference ('pro' means forward) earlier learning disrupts later learning: the old dog's old tricks block the new ones. In retroactive interference ('retro' means backward) later learning disrupts recall of earlier material. Benton Underwood (1957) showed how strong the proactive kind is: people who had already learned many lists in the laboratory forgot a new list far more over a day than people learning their first list, so much of the forgetting came from the old lists, not from time. Example: Arjun has typed on a QWERTY keyboard for twenty years; when he tries a new layout, his fingers keep hitting the old keys. That is proactive interference. If, after months on the new layout, he can no longer type well on QWERTY, that is retroactive interference. Option c is right because the saying describes old habits (earlier learning) blocking the learning of new tricks, which is the forward, old-disrupts-new direction. Exam trap: the two terms are easy to reverse; remember PRO = old disrupts new and RETRO = new disrupts old, then ask which learning is suffering. Here it is the new learning, so the answer is proactive.", "topic": "Proactive versus retroactive interference", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The overriding Gestalt principle is:", "options": {"a": "Closure", "b": "Similarity", "c": "Proximity", "d": "Simplicity"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the overriding Gestalt principle is simplicity, the law of Pragnanz (good figure). Gestalt psychology held that the whole we perceive is different from the sum of its parts, and that the visual system actively organises what reaches the eye. Max Wertheimer (1923) described the laws by which elements are grouped: proximity (things near each other go together), similarity (things alike in colour, shape or size go together), closure (gaps are filled in to make a complete figure), good continuation (smooth lines are followed) and common fate (things moving together belong together). Kurt Koffka, in Principles of Gestalt Psychology (1935), then treated Pragnanz as the master law: of all the ways a pattern could be organised, we see the simplest, most regular and most stable one the conditions allow. The other laws are particular cases of it. Closure, for instance, works because a whole circle is a simpler figure than a broken arc. Example: Meera sees the Olympic logo as five overlapping rings rather than as a dozen odd curved pieces, because rings are the simplest reading of the pattern. Option d is right because simplicity (good form) is the principle that sits above and explains the other grouping laws, which is why textbooks call it the overriding or fundamental Gestalt law. Exam trap: closure, similarity and proximity are all real Gestalt laws, so each looks correct; the stem asks for the one that governs them all, and that is Pragnanz, also called the law of good figure or the law of simplicity.", "topic": "Gestalt law of Pragnanz", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Although STM stores information in terms of physical qualities, LTM stores information in terms of:", "options": {"a": "Acoustic codes", "b": "Semantic codes", "c": "Attitudes", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: long-term memory (LTM) stores information mainly in terms of semantic codes, that is, by meaning. In the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), information passes from sensory memory to short-term memory (STM), which holds a few items for about 18 to 30 seconds, and, with rehearsal, into LTM, which is large and lasting. The stores also differ in how they code material. STM leans on physical codes, mostly acoustic (how words sound), while LTM leans on meaning. Alan Baddeley (1966) showed this with word lists. Lists of similar-sounding words (man, mad, cap, can) were hard to recall straight away, so STM was confused by sound. Lists of similar-meaning words (big, huge, large, wide) were hard to recall after a delay, so LTM was confused by meaning. Example: a week after a lecture, Riya can tell you the gist of what was said, but not the exact words; she has kept the meaning and lost the sound. LTM can hold sounds and images too (you know a song's tune and your mother's face), but meaning is its dominant code. Option b is right because semantic coding is the typical form of storage in LTM, in contrast to the physical (acoustic or visual) coding of STM that the stem mentions. Exam trap: acoustic codes belong mainly to STM, the very store the stem contrasts LTM with; 'All of the above' fails because LTM is described by its dominant code, and attitudes are not a memory code at all.", "topic": "Semantic coding in LTM", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What are the functions of cues in discrimination learning?", "options": {"a": "They change the circuit architecture of brain, fostering learning", "b": "They act as agents of forgetting", "c": "They serve as distracters, inhibiting the effects of discrimination learning", "d": "They act as signals as to what behaviors will be reinforced or punished"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: in discrimination learning, cues act as signals telling the learner which behaviour will be reinforced or punished. B. F. Skinner (1938) called such a cue a discriminative stimulus (S-D): a stimulus in whose presence a response has been reinforced, so the response becomes more likely whenever it appears. A stimulus signalling that the response will not be reinforced is an S-delta; one signalling that it will be punished is a discriminative stimulus for punishment. When behaviour occurs in the presence of the S-D but not of the S-delta, it is under stimulus control. This is how discrimination is learned: the organism comes to respond differently to different stimuli because they predict different consequences. In the laboratory, a pigeon rewarded for pecking only when a key is lit green soon pecks at green and ignores red. Example: Kabir has learned that asking for sweets works when his grandmother is at home but earns a scolding when his father is there; his grandmother's presence is the S-D and his father's is a discriminative stimulus for punishment. The opposite process is generalisation, responding in the same way to similar stimuli. Option d is right because the whole function of a discriminative cue is to carry information about likely consequences, so that the right behaviour appears in the right setting. Exam trap: do not confuse discrimination with generalisation, and note that the cue does not elicit the response automatically, as a conditioned stimulus does in Ivan Pavlov's (1927) classical conditioning; it only sets the occasion for a response whose consequences have been learned.", "topic": "Discriminative stimuli", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Each of the following is a belief of stage theorist except:", "options": {"a": "As children progress through the stages, the differences between children are qualitative", "b": "As children progress through the stages, the differences are quantitative", "c": "Children pass through the same stages through the same order", "d": "Stages are biologically programmed to unfold"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: stage theorists do not hold that the differences between stages are merely quantitative; that is the continuity view. Developmental psychology asks whether change is continuous (gradual and quantitative, like a ramp: more knowledge, faster processing, a bigger vocabulary) or discontinuous (a series of qualitatively different stages, like a staircase). Stage theorists, such as Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) for cognition, Sigmund Freud (1905 to 1923) for psychosexual development, Erik Erikson (1950) for psychosocial development and Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) for moral reasoning, take the discontinuous side. They share three beliefs. First, each stage is qualitatively different: a child at a new stage thinks in a new way, not just more of the old way. Second, every child passes through the same stages in the same invariant order, in every culture. Third, the stages are largely set by biological maturation and unfold on a built-in timetable; experience can speed or slow them, but not reorder them. Example: when Arjun, at about 7, realises that water poured into a tall thin glass is still the same amount, Piaget would say his thinking has changed in kind, not that he simply knows more facts. Learning theorists and information-processing theorists, by contrast, see development as continuous, quantitative growth. Option b is right because 'the differences are quantitative' is the hallmark of the continuity view, so it is the one statement a stage theorist would reject. Exam trap: options a and b differ by one word; qualitative change means a new kind of thinking (the stage view), while quantitative change means more of the same (the continuity view).", "topic": "Stage theories of development", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is characteristic of pre-operational child?", "options": {"a": "The child is ego centric", "b": "The child uses transductive reasoning", "c": "The child is capable of abstract thought", "d": "(A) and (B) above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: the preoperational child is both egocentric and uses transductive reasoning, so (A) and (B) is correct. In Jean Piaget's (1896 to 1980) theory, the preoperational stage runs from about 2 to 7 years. The child can now use symbols (words, images, pretend play) but cannot yet perform operations, mental actions that are logical and reversible. Several limits follow. Egocentrism is the failure to take another person's point of view. In the three mountains task, Jean Piaget and Barbel Inhelder (1956) asked children to pick the picture showing what a doll on the far side of a model could see; four-year-olds usually chose their own view. Transductive reasoning runs from one particular event to another, with no general rule, so two things that happen together are treated as cause and effect. Piaget's own daughter, having missed her nap, insisted that it could not be afternoon. Other features are centration, animism, irreversibility and the lack of conservation. Example: five-year-old Meera thinks her baby brother fell ill because she was cross with him (transductive), and she hides by covering her eyes, sure that if she cannot see you, you cannot see her (egocentric). Option d is right because both A and B are classic preoperational features, while C belongs to a later stage. Exam trap: abstract, hypothetical thought appears only in the formal operational stage, at about 11 years and above; picking A or B alone misses that both are true.", "topic": "Preoperational thought", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "A research study in a realistic situation in which one or more independent variables are manipulated by the experimenter under as carefully controlled conditions as the situation permits is known as:", "options": {"a": "A field experiment", "b": "A situation experiment", "c": "A case study", "d": "Observation study"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the stem is the classic definition of a field experiment. Fred Kerlinger, in Foundations of Behavioral Research (first edition 1964), defined it almost word for word: a research study in a realistic situation in which one or more independent variables (IVs) are manipulated by the experimenter under as carefully controlled conditions as the situation permits. Two features matter. First, it is a true experiment: the researcher manipulates the IV and, where possible, assigns people to conditions at random, so cause and effect can be inferred. Second, it happens in the real world (a train, a school, a market), where people often do not know they are being studied. This gives higher external validity and fewer demand characteristics than a laboratory experiment, at the cost of less control over extraneous variables. The classic example is the New York subway study of Irving Piliavin, Judith Rodin and Jane Piliavin (1969): a confederate collapsed in a carriage, appearing either ill (carrying a cane) or drunk, and observers recorded who helped and how fast. Example: Kabir, a school psychologist, has two sections of Class 8 use different revision methods for a month and compares their marks; he manipulates the IV inside a real school. Option a is right because the stem names both defining elements: a realistic setting and a manipulated IV with as much control as the setting allows. Exam trap: a field study also takes place in a natural setting but manipulates nothing; the manipulation is what makes it an experiment.", "topic": "Field experiment", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What BEST distinguishes the infancy stage from other stages of development?", "options": {"a": "Physical growth is most rapid in the first year", "b": "Cognitive growth is 5 times greater than in any other stage", "c": "Emotions are fully developed before the next development stage", "d": "It is the only stage that has no emotional development"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: what best distinguishes infancy is that physical growth is more rapid in the first year than at any later stage. A newborn's weight roughly doubles by about 5 months and triples by the first birthday, and body length rises by about half in the first year. No later period comes close; even the adolescent growth spurt adds far less in relative terms. Growth in infancy follows two trends. In the cephalocaudal trend (head to tail) the head grows first and fastest, which is why a baby's head is about a quarter of its body length. In the proximodistal trend growth runs from the trunk outward to the arms, hands and fingers. Example: baby Riya weighs 3 kg at birth, about 6 kg at 5 months and about 9 kg on her first birthday; no later year of her life will see her weight triple. Option a is right because rapid physical growth is the defining feature of the first year after birth, while each of the other options makes a false claim. Exam trap: infancy is also a period of rich emotional development (the social smile at about 6 to 8 weeks, stranger anxiety, attachment), so any option saying emotions are absent in infancy, or finished by its end, is wrong. Key note: growth is faster still before birth, in the prenatal period, so the claim holds for the stages after birth; a stands.", "topic": "Infant physical growth", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Using Jung's theory, 'a wild man' professional wrestler would be seen as an expression of:", "options": {"a": "The id", "b": "The anima", "c": "The wild man archetype", "d": "A harsh upbringing"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the theory of Carl Jung (1875 to 1961), a 'wild man' wrestler would be read as an expression of the wild man archetype. Jung broke with Sigmund Freud in 1913 and divided the unconscious into two layers. The personal unconscious holds one person's forgotten and repressed experiences. Beneath it lies the collective unconscious, inherited and shared by all humans, the deposit of our ancestors' experience. Its contents are archetypes: universal, inborn patterns (a readiness to respond, not inherited memories) that show up in myths, fairy tales, dreams and popular culture across the world. Jung's best-known archetypes are the persona (social mask), the shadow (the dark, animal side), the anima (the feminine side of a man), the animus (the masculine side of a woman) and the self (wholeness), along with the hero, the great mother and the trickster. Later Jungian writers, notably Robert Bly in Iron John (1990), described the Wild Man as an archetype of untamed masculine energy. Example: Kabir cheers a wrestler who storms into the ring wild-haired and roaring; a Jungian would say the act grips crowds everywhere because it taps a universal image. Option c is right because Jung explains such recurring, culture-wide figures as expressions of archetypes in the collective unconscious. Exam trap: the stem says 'using Jung's theory', so Freudian (id) and environmental (upbringing) answers are out, and the anima, though Jungian, is the feminine side of a man. Key note: 'wild man' is not among Jung's best-known named archetypes, and a shadow reading is also possible, but of these options only c names a Jungian archetype; c stands.", "topic": "Jung's archetypes", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "Cattell believed that pathological personalities are of two kinds; those with one or more abnormal traits and those:", "options": {"a": "Who are born defective", "b": "With an extreme imbalance of normal traits", "c": "Without any normal traits", "d": "Who have inadequate defense mechanism"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Raymond B. Cattell held that pathological personalities either have one or more abnormal traits or have normal traits at an extreme, unbalanced level. Raymond B. Cattell (1905 to 1998) used factor analysis to find source traits, the deep, stable factors beneath behaviour; 16 of them make up his Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949). He then factor-analysed clinical as well as normal samples and concluded that disturbed people differ from normal people in two ways. Some have the normal traits plus one or more abnormal (pathological) traits that normal people lack; textbook accounts count 12 such factors, seven of them kinds of depression, plus others such as paranoia, psychopathic deviation and schizophrenia. Others have no extra trait at all: their pathology lies in normal traits carried to an extreme, or in an imbalance among them. Cattell's Clinical Analysis Questionnaire (CAQ; first manual 1971, later developed with Samuel Krug) measures both sets, the 16 normal factors and the 12 clinical ones. Example: Arjun is extremely high on apprehension and extremely low on emotional stability; each trait is normal in itself, but the extreme combination is disabling. Option b is right because the second kind of pathological personality in Cattell's scheme is the person with an extreme imbalance of normal traits. Exam trap: Cattell's model is dimensional and statistical, so do not import Freudian ideas (defence mechanisms) or a 'born defective' notion into it; and pathological people keep their normal traits rather than lacking them.", "topic": "Cattell on abnormal traits", "fig": "Raymond B. Cattell"}, {"q": "Sems are:", "options": {"a": "Learned or acquired traits", "b": "Innate drives or motives", "c": "Fourth order traits", "d": "Ability traits"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: sems are learned or acquired traits. Raymond B. Cattell (1905 to 1998) sorted traits by function into ability traits (how well we pursue goals, such as intelligence), temperament traits (emotional style) and dynamic traits (what moves us to act). Dynamic traits come in two main kinds. Ergs are innate, biological drives that humans share with other animals, such as hunger, sex, fear, curiosity, gregariousness and self-assertion. Sentiments, which Cattell himself shortened to sems (as do textbooks such as Jess Feist and Gregory Feist's Theories of Personality), are learned patterns of attitudes built around an important object or institution, such as a career, religion, the family, the country or the self. They are environmental-mould traits, formed by experience, and one sem can satisfy several ergs at once. The most important is the self-sentiment, which integrates the others. Cattell linked the levels by subsidiation: specific attitudes serve sentiments, and sentiments serve ergs. The whole web of links is the dynamic lattice. Example: Meera wants to revise tonight (an attitude); this serves her career sentiment (a sem), which in turn serves her ergs of self-assertion and security. Option a is right because sems are the acquired, learned side of Cattell's dynamic traits, in contrast to the inborn ergs. Exam trap: examiners swap the two terms; ergs are innate (think biological energy) and sems are learned; and both are dynamic traits, not ability traits.", "topic": "Cattell's ergs and sems", "fig": "Raymond B. Cattell"}, {"q": "The theory of Cattell rates:", "options": {"a": "High on biological determinants of personality", "b": "High on teleology", "c": "Low on their ability to generate research", "d": "High on free will"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: Cattell's theory rates high on biological determinants of personality. Personality theory textbooks rate each theory on its concept of humanity (free will versus determinism, causality versus teleology, biological versus social influences and so on) and on its scientific usefulness. Raymond B. Cattell (1905 to 1998) built his theory from factor analysis and behaviour genetics. Using his multiple abstract variance analysis (MAVA), which compares twins and siblings reared together and apart, he estimated how much of each trait is inherited, and he reported substantial heritability for several traits and especially high values for intelligence. He also split source traits by origin into constitutional traits (rooted in biology) and environmental-mould traits (learned). So the theory gives heredity a strong role and rates high, even very high, on biological influences. Its picture of people is deterministic: the specification equation predicts behaviour from a person's traits weighted by the situation, so free will and teleology (future goals pulling behaviour forward) play little or no part. On usefulness, the theory rates high on generating research, because its precise, measurable factors and the 16PF led to a large body of studies. Example: if Kabir and his identical twin, raised in different cities, both score high on boldness, Cattell would read this as evidence of a genetic contribution. Option a is right because heavy reliance on heredity and biology is exactly how the theory is rated. Exam trap: teleology and free will are emphases of humanistic and Adlerian theories, and the research rating for factor theories is high, not low.", "topic": "Evaluating Cattell's theory", "fig": "Raymond B. Cattell"}, {"q": "Which of these can not be used in a Likert scale?", "options": {"a": "Agree", "b": "Totally agree", "c": "Neither agree nor disagree", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: none of these labels is barred, so every listed response can be used in a Likert scale. Rensis Likert (1932) introduced the method of summated ratings. A Likert scale is a set of statements about an attitude object; for each statement the respondent chooses one of a fixed number of ordered options showing how far they agree. The classic set has five points: strongly agree, agree, neither agree nor disagree (undecided), disagree and strongly disagree, scored 5 to 1 (reversed for unfavourable statements). The item scores are then summed into one attitude score. The anchor wording is flexible. 'Agree' is a standard point. 'Neither agree nor disagree' is the usual neutral midpoint; some researchers drop it to force a choice (a 4-point or 6-point scale), but it is allowed and common. The top anchor is often written 'totally agree' or 'completely agree' instead of 'strongly agree', especially in translated questionnaires, and Likert's own 1932 scales used strongly approve, approve, undecided, disapprove and strongly disapprove. Example: Riya builds a scale on attitudes to online classes with the anchors totally disagree, disagree, neither agree nor disagree, agree and totally agree; it is still a Likert scale, because the format is ordered agreement and the scores are summed. Option d is right because agree, totally agree and neither agree nor disagree can all serve as Likert responses, so none of them is excluded. Exam trap: do not pick the neutral midpoint just because some scales leave it out; dropping it is a design choice, not a rule. Key note: some readers argue that 'totally agree' is not a standard Likert label and so pick b, but the item hinges on 'totally agree' being accepted as a variant of 'strongly agree', which it widely is; d stands.", "topic": "Likert scale response labels", "fig": "Rensis Likert"}, {"q": "Which of these isn't attitude statement?", "options": {"a": "Somehow, I don't regard myself as an achiever", "b": "Some people like to bully others", "c": "I love sweets", "d": "I wish I could be more helpful"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: 'Some people like to bully others' is not an attitude statement. An attitude is an evaluative disposition (a liking or disliking, with its beliefs and action tendencies) towards an object, person, group or idea. An attitude statement must therefore let respondents reveal their own evaluation: whether they agree should depend on how they feel. Allen Edwards (1957), in Techniques of Attitude Scale Construction, gathered rules for writing such statements. Among them: avoid statements of fact, or statements that could be read as factual; avoid statements that almost everyone or almost no one would endorse; refer to the present rather than the past; keep statements short, clear and simple, with one idea each. 'Some people like to bully others' breaks the first rule. It is a factual claim about other people, true as a matter of observation, so a kind person and a bully would both agree with it, and the answer tells you nothing about the respondent's own attitude to bullying. Compare 'Bullying is sometimes justified', which does reveal an attitude. Example: Kabir, writing a scale on attitudes to bullying for Class 9 students, drops this item at the tryout stage because everyone ticks 'agree'. Option b is right because it states a fact about others rather than the respondent's own feeling, belief or intention. Exam trap: first-person wording is a clue, but the real test is evaluation; 'I love sweets' and 'I wish I could be more helpful' express how the speaker feels, while a general observation about the world does not.", "topic": "Writing attitude statements", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Research participants who are shown pictures of extremely attractive individuals and then are asked to rate another person, often show a(n):", "options": {"a": "Rating discrepancy", "b": "Contrast effect", "c": "Latency effect", "d": "Associated effect"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: people who first see very attractive individuals and then rate someone else show a contrast effect. A contrast effect is a judgement bias in which a stimulus is judged against what came just before it: after something extreme, an ordinary stimulus seems further from it than it really is. It runs through perception (lukewarm water feels cold after hot water) and social judgement alike. Douglas Kenrick and Sara Gutierres (1980) tested it on attractiveness. Male students who were watching Charlie's Angels, a television show starring three glamorous actresses, rated a photo of an average-looking woman, offered as a possible blind date, as less attractive than men who had not been watching. Douglas Kenrick, Sara Gutierres and Laurie Goldberg (1989) reported that men who looked at centrefold photographs then rated their own partners as less attractive and said they loved them less, though later, much larger replications did not find this partner effect. Example: Arjun scrolls through photos of film stars for half an hour and then finds his friend's wedding photos oddly ordinary; the faces have not changed, his comparison standard has. Option b is right because the lowered rating comes from comparing the new person with the extremely attractive images just seen. Exam trap: do not confuse the contrast effect with the halo effect, in which one good trait (such as good looks) makes us rate a person well on unrelated traits; a halo spreads a judgement, whereas contrast pushes it away from a reference point.", "topic": "Contrast effect in attractiveness", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If there was no CO₂ in the atmosphere, the earth's atmosphere would be:", "options": {"a": "Less than the present", "b": "Same as present", "c": "Higher than present", "d": "Dependent on oxygen content of air"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: without carbon dioxide (CO2) the earth's temperature would be lower than at present. The ground, warmed by sunlight, gives off heat as infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases (water vapour, CO2, methane) absorb much of this outgoing heat and send part of it back down. John Tyndall (1859) showed in the laboratory that CO2 absorbs heat, and Svante Arrhenius (1896) calculated how changes in CO2 would change the earth's temperature. Without any greenhouse gases, the average surface temperature would be about minus 18 degrees Celsius instead of about plus 15. CO2 matters more than its small share suggests: warmer air holds more water vapour, so removing the CO2 would let much of the water vapour condense out, and the planet would cool sharply. Option a is right because less CO2 means a weaker greenhouse effect and a colder earth. Exam trap: CO2's warming does not depend on oxygen. Key note: the stem is garbled and says 'atmosphere' where it means temperature; read as the earth's temperature, a stands.", "topic": "Greenhouse effect of CO2", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What are the main components of Spearman's two factor theory?", "options": {"a": "Specific intelligence and general intelligence", "b": "Visual ability and spatial ability", "c": "Primary abilities and secondary abilities", "d": "Emotion abilities and cognitive abilities"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Spearman's two-factor theory has two components, general intelligence (g) and specific intelligence (s). Charles Spearman (1904), in his paper 'General intelligence, objectively determined and measured', noticed that schoolchildren's scores on very different measures (classics, French, mathematics, pitch discrimination) all correlated positively, a pattern now called the positive manifold. Using an early form of factor analysis, he explained it by a general factor, g, shared by all mental tasks and pictured as a kind of mental energy, plus a specific factor, s, unique to each test. Every test score therefore reflects g plus that test's own s. Tests that draw heavily on g, such as reasoning, correlate strongly with one another; tests that rely more on s, such as simple sensory tasks, correlate less. Spearman developed the theory in The Abilities of Man (1927). Example: Riya does well in mathematics, English and science alike, which Spearman would credit to a high g, while her outstanding skill at drawing reflects a specific factor. Option a is right because the two factors in the theory's name are exactly general intelligence and specific intelligence. Exam trap: primary mental abilities belong to Louis Leon Thurstone (1938), who found about seven group factors and challenged a single g; broad group factors such as verbal-educational and spatial-mechanical ability belong to Philip Vernon's (1950) hierarchical model.", "topic": "Spearman's two-factor theory", "fig": "Charles Spearman"}, {"q": "How many SD above the mean IQ score did Terman and Oden start to assess individuals as being gifted?", "options": {"a": "1 SD above the mean IQ score", "b": "No SD above the mean IQ score", "c": "3 SD above the mean IQ score", "d": "2 SD above the mean IQ score"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: giftedness is conventionally set at 2 standard deviations (SD) above the mean IQ, an IQ of about 130. Lewis Terman (1877 to 1956), who produced the Stanford-Binet test (1916), began his Genetic Studies of Genius in 1921. He identified 1,528 California children with very high Stanford-Binet IQs, mostly 135 to 140 and above (mean about 151), and followed them for the rest of their lives; they became known as the 'Termites'. Melita Oden co-wrote the follow-ups, The Gifted Child Grows Up (1947) and The Gifted Group at Mid-Life (1959). The study overturned the myth that bright children are frail and odd: as adults they were, on average, healthier, better adjusted and more successful than others. On modern tests with a mean of 100 and an SD of 15, an IQ of 130 lies 2 SD above the mean and is reached by only about 2 per cent of people; IQ 70, 2 SD below the mean, marks the range of intellectual disability. Example: Meera scores 132 on a Wechsler scale; at more than 2 SD above the mean, she would usually qualify as gifted. Option d is right because 2 SD above the mean is the textbook gifted cut-off linked to Terman and Oden's group. Exam trap: 1 SD above (IQ 115) is merely above average, and 3 SD above (IQ 145) is far rarer, about 0.1 per cent of people. Key note: Terman's own cut-off was about IQ 135 to 140, between 2 and 2.5 SD, so '2 SD' is the textbook rounding and the closest option; d stands.", "topic": "Terman's gifted cut-off", "fig": "Lewis Terman and Melita Oden"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is most consistent with the two factor theory of emotion?", "options": {"a": "Physiological arousal can be interpreted in different ways resulting in different emotions", "b": "Physiological arousal and emotion happen simultaneously", "c": "Physiological arousal happens first, emotion then follows", "d": "Emotion happens first, physiological arousal then follows"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the two-factor theory holds that the same physiological arousal can be interpreted in different ways, producing different emotions. Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962) proposed that an emotion has two ingredients: physiological arousal, which sets how strong the feeling is, and a cognitive label taken from the situation, which decides which emotion it is. Because arousal (racing heart, sweating, trembling) is much the same in fear, anger and joy, the label does the work of telling them apart. In their classic experiment, students were injected with adrenaline (epinephrine), described as a vitamin; some were told its real effects, others were told nothing or misinformed, and a placebo group got saline. Each then waited with a stooge who acted either euphoric or angry. The aroused but uninformed students, who had no explanation for their racing hearts, caught the stooge's mood most, while the informed students, who could blame the injection, did so least. Example: Arjun's heart is pounding after he runs up four flights of stairs; if he meets a friend who is laughing, he may feel elated, but if someone shouts at him, the same arousal may feel like anger. Option a is right because it states the core claim: one state of arousal, different interpretations, different emotions. Exam trap: match each sequence to its theory; arousal and emotion at the same time is Cannon-Bard, arousal first and then emotion is James-Lange, and emotion first and then arousal is the common-sense view.", "topic": "Two-factor theory of emotion", "fig": "Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer"}, {"q": "Thorndike's Multifactor theory of intelligence is at one extreme of the interpretations regarding the nature of:", "options": {"a": "Motor organizations", "b": "Intellectual organizations", "c": "Mental organizations", "d": "Reasoning"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Edward Thorndike's (1927) multifactor theory sits at one extreme of the interpretations regarding the nature of mental organisation, that is, how the abilities that make up intelligence are structured. Theorists who correlated many tests asked whether the abilities behind them form one general power, a few broad group factors or a host of separate bits. Thorndike gave the most splintered answer. For him intelligence is made up of a multitude of separate, minute elements of ability, each a set of stimulus-response connections. Any mental act uses a number of these elements together, two tasks correlate only to the extent that they share elements, and there is no general intelligence: a brighter person simply has more connections. The theory is therefore called atomistic. Even so, Thorndike grouped tasks into abstract, mechanical and social intelligence. At the other extreme stands Charles Spearman's (1904) two-factor theory, in which all intellectual activity depends mainly on one general factor, g, plus a specific factor, s, for each task; group-factor theories such as that of Louis Leon Thurstone (1938) sit in between. Example: Kabir repairs engines brilliantly but is average at abstract puzzles; Thorndike would call these largely separate abilities. Option c is right because the range from multifactor to two-factor theories is a range of views on how the mind's abilities are organised, which is exactly the phrase the textbook uses. Exam trap: the opposite pole is Spearman's g, not Thurstone's primary mental abilities, which are a middle position; and do not mix up Thorndike (multifactor) with Thurstone (primary mental abilities). Key note: the stem copies a sentence from Frank S. Freeman's Theory and Practice of Psychological Testing (third edition, 1962), which reads 'the nature of mental organization', so c is the answer the paper intends. Option b, intellectual organisation, means much the same thing here; if you picked it you understood the idea, but on this paper c is the safe mark, and the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Thorndike's multifactor theory", "fig": "Edward Thorndike"}, {"q": "In interpersonal communication ethics:", "options": {"a": "Are not a consideration", "b": "Increase barriers to understanding", "c": "Are important", "d": "Stand in the way of honesty"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: in interpersonal communication, ethics are important. Communication ethics are the moral standards for how we speak to, listen to and treat one another: being honest, respecting the other person's dignity and right to choose, keeping confidences, giving accurate information and not manipulating, deceiving or bullying. The National Communication Association's Credo for Ethical Communication (1999) puts truthfulness, accuracy, honesty and reason at the centre of good communication, and asks us to understand and respect other communicators before judging their messages. Ethical communication builds trust, and trust lowers the barriers (suspicion, defensiveness, hidden agendas) that block understanding. Example: Meera tells her friend Kabir honestly that she cannot lend him money, instead of inventing an excuse; the honesty keeps the friendship open and trusting. Option c is right because ethics decide whether communication is truthful, respectful and trusted. Exam trap: the three wrong options all reverse the point by calling ethics irrelevant or a hindrance; in fact ethics support honesty and understanding rather than standing in their way.", "topic": "Ethics in interpersonal communication", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Legitimate power is based on the subordinate's perception that the leader has a right to exercise influence because of the leader's:", "options": {"a": "Role or position within the organization", "b": "Personal characteristics and personality", "c": "Expertise and knowledge", "d": "Ability to punish or reward"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: legitimate power rests on the leader's role or position within the organisation. John French and Bertram Raven (1959) described five bases of social power, the sources from which one person's capacity to influence another comes. Legitimate power comes from an accepted right to give orders that goes with a role: subordinates comply because they believe a manager, officer or teacher is entitled to direct them, whoever holds the post. Reward power rests on the ability to give rewards (bonuses, praise), coercive power on the ability to punish, expert power on superior knowledge or skill, and referent power on admiration and identification with the agent. Raven (1965) later added informational power, the persuasive content of the message itself. Legitimate power goes when the role goes: a retired principal cannot order the staff about. In Stanley Milgram's (1963) obedience studies, the experimenter's authority as a scientist in a laboratory worked largely through legitimate power. Example: Arjun submits his report by Friday because his team leader, Priya, asked him to and setting deadlines is part of her job, not because he likes her or fears a penalty. Option a is right because the stem's phrase 'a right to exercise influence' is the definition of legitimate power, and that right comes from the position. Exam trap: a manager usually holds several bases at once, but the 'right' to influence is legitimate power; the ability to reward or punish (option d) is a separate pair of bases, even though it often comes with the position.", "topic": "French and Raven's legitimate power", "fig": "John French and Bertram Raven"}, {"q": "Behaviours that are persistently aggressive or involve acting out and non compliant behaviours are _____ behaviours.", "options": {"a": "Externalizing", "b": "Internalizing", "c": "Defiant", "d": "Antisocial"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: persistently aggressive, acting-out and non-compliant behaviours are called externalising behaviours. Thomas Achenbach (1966), factor-analysing the recorded symptoms of child psychiatric patients, found that children's problems fall into two broad groups. Externalising problems are undercontrolled and directed outward, at other people and the environment: aggression, defiance, temper outbursts, rule-breaking, lying, stealing and hyperactivity. They form the core of oppositional defiant disorder, conduct disorder and, in part, ADHD. Internalising problems are overcontrolled and directed inward, at the self: anxiety, depression, withdrawal, shyness and bodily complaints. Both broad-band dimensions are scored on Achenbach's Child Behavior Checklist, completed by parents, with parallel forms for teachers (the Teacher's Report Form) and for adolescents themselves (the Youth Self-Report). Externalising problems trouble the people around the child, so they are referred more often; internalising problems mainly trouble the child and are easily missed. Example: Rohan, aged 9, hits classmates, refuses the teacher's instructions and throws things when told off, an externalising pattern; his sister Anika, quietly anxious and tearful, shows an internalising one. Option a is right because the stem describes the outward-directed, acting-out cluster, which is the definition of externalising behaviour. Exam trap: 'defiant' and 'antisocial' sound right because the stem mentions non-compliance and aggression, but they are narrower labels (oppositional defiant disorder; conduct disorder and antisocial personality disorder) that sit inside the broader externalising dimension.", "topic": "Externalising versus internalising behaviour", "fig": "Thomas Achenbach"}, {"q": "A common feature of panic attacks is hyperventilation and it is due to:", "options": {"a": "Dysfunctional breathing patterns", "b": "Raising blood pH level", "c": "Oxygen is then delivered less effectively to body cells", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements describe the hyperventilation that so often comes with panic attacks. Hyperventilation means over-breathing, breathing faster or deeper than the body needs: a dysfunctional breathing pattern (option a). Breathing out too much carbon dioxide lowers its level in the blood (hypocapnia). Carbon dioxide forms carbonic acid, so losing it makes the blood less acidic: blood pH rises, a state called respiratory alkalosis (option b). Through the Bohr effect, haemoglobin then holds on to oxygen more tightly, and blood vessels in the brain narrow, so oxygen is delivered less effectively to the body's cells (option c). The result is dizziness, light-headedness, tingling in the fingers and lips, breathlessness, a racing heart and chest tightness. In David Clark's (1986) cognitive model of panic, the person catastrophically misinterprets these harmless sensations as a heart attack, suffocation or going mad, which raises anxiety and over-breathing further in a vicious circle. Treatment therefore includes breathing retraining and interoceptive exposure, in which the client over-breathes on purpose to learn that the sensations are safe. Example: Neha, in a crowded metro, starts gulping air, feels her fingers tingle and her head spin, and is sure she is dying. Option d is right because the dysfunctional breathing pattern, the rise in pH and the poorer oxygen delivery are all parts of the same hyperventilation picture. Exam trap: many students pick a alone, but b and c are also textbook statements about hyperventilation, so 'All of the above' is the complete answer. Key note: strictly, raised blood pH and poorer oxygen delivery are consequences of hyperventilation rather than its causes, so the stem's 'due to' is loose; the item bundles the cause and the effects of the same breathing problem, and the answer used here, d, stands.", "topic": "Hyperventilation in panic attacks", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a predominant evolutionary theory of phobias?", "options": {"a": "Nonassociative fear acquisition", "b": "Learned fear response", "c": "Biological preparedness", "d": "Specific phobic acquisition"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: biological preparedness is the leading evolutionary theory of phobias. Martin Seligman (1971) argued that evolution has prepared humans to learn fears of things that threatened our ancestors, such as snakes, spiders, heights, deep water and angry faces, far more easily than fears of modern dangers such as electric sockets or cars. Prepared fears can form after a single bad experience, or even from watching someone else, and they resist extinction. This explains why phobias cluster around a short list of ancestral objects. Strong evidence comes from rhesus monkeys: Michael Cook and Susan Mineka (1989) showed laboratory-reared monkeys videos of other monkeys reacting with fear; they learned to fear toy snakes and toy crocodiles, but not flowers or toy rabbits edited into the same footage. Arne Ohman and Susan Mineka (2001) later described an evolved \"fear module\" in the brain that such cues trigger selectively. Example: Riya has never been bitten, yet one scream from her cousin at a garden snake leaves her with a lasting snake phobia, while a far more dangerous shock from a faulty switch leaves no phobia at all. Option c is right because preparedness is the evolutionary explanation that dominates textbook accounts of phobias. Exam trap: a learned fear response (option b) sounds scientific but is plain conditioning with no evolutionary claim, while the non-associative account (option a) is also evolutionary, so students who spot only that word may pick it. Key note: Ross Menzies and John Clarke (1995) proposed non-associative fear acquisition (reviewed by Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies, 2002), in which some fears (of heights, water, strangers) are innate and appear without any learning; it is evolutionary too, but a minority view, and preparedness remains the predominant evolutionary account, so c stands.", "topic": "Preparedness theory of phobias", "fig": "Martin Seligman"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is most consistent with the biopsychosocial model?", "options": {"a": "Mental illness is caused by brain abnormalities, neurochemical abnormalities and genetic influences", "b": "Societal forces such as oppression and poverty drive the development of mental illness", "c": "Genetic heritage causes a predisposition to mental illness, but environment and cognitive/emotional factors must be present for mental illness to develop", "d": "Mental illness does not exist but is rather a normal reaction to an abnormal situation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: the statement that a genetic predisposition leads to mental illness only when environmental and cognitive or emotional factors are also present is the one most consistent with the biopsychosocial model. The psychiatrist George Engel (1977), in a paper in Science, argued that the purely biomedical model cannot explain why people with the same biochemical abnormality differ so much in whether, and how badly, they fall ill. He proposed that every illness arises from interacting biological factors (genes, brain chemistry), psychological factors (thoughts, emotions, coping, learning) and social factors (family, poverty, culture, life stress), so treatment must address all three levels. Option c is the diathesis-stress form of this idea: Paul Meehl (1962), and later Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977), held that an inherited vulnerability (the diathesis) lies dormant until stress triggers it, and neither alone is enough. Example: Kavya has a family history of depression (biological), tends to blame herself for every setback (psychological) and loses her job and her support network in the same year (social); the model says her depression comes from all of these together. Option c is right because it is the only option that combines biological, psychological and social causes and says none alone is sufficient. Exam trap: options a and b both sound scientific, but any statement that blames one level only (genes and brain alone, or society alone) is a single-factor model; the biopsychosocial answer is always the one that combines the levels.", "topic": "Biopsychosocial model of illness", "fig": "George Engel"}, {"q": "In an experiment an effect size is:", "options": {"a": "The success of experimental manipulation", "b": "An objective and standardized measure of the magnitude of the effect observed in a study", "c": "Effective hypothesis testing", "d": "Measurement of how successfully you carried out the experiment"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: an effect size is an objective and standardised measure of the magnitude of the effect observed in a study. A significance test tells you whether an effect is probably not zero, but the p value depends heavily on sample size: with thousands of participants even a trivial difference becomes 'significant'. An effect size answers the other question, how much, in a unit that does not depend on N and can be compared across studies. The best known is Cohen's d, the difference between two group means divided by their pooled standard deviation; Jacob Cohen (1988) suggested 0.2 as small, 0.5 as medium and 0.8 as large. Other indices are the correlation r (.10, .30 and .50 as small, medium and large) and eta squared, the share of variance an independent variable explains in ANOVA. Effect sizes are also the raw material of meta-analysis, the method Gene Glass (1976) named for combining the results of many studies. Example: Riya's mindfulness group scores 5 points lower on anxiety than her control group, and the pooled SD is 10, so d = 0.5, a medium effect, whatever her p value. Option b is right because it gives the textbook definition: standardised, objective and about the size of the observed effect. Exam trap: effect size is not the same as statistical significance or power, and it says nothing about whether the experiment was run well; options a, c and d all describe the success or quality of the study instead of the size of the effect.", "topic": "Effect size", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A blind experiment is one in which:", "options": {"a": "Only the subjects, not the administrator, know who is receiving the treatment", "b": "Subjects and administrators know who is receiving the treatment", "c": "Neither the subjects nor the administrators of the treatment know which group is receiving the treatment", "d": "Subjects do not know if they are in the experimental or control group"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: in a blind experiment, the subjects do not know whether they are in the experimental or the control group. Blinding (masking) keeps knowledge of the condition from people whose expectations could bias the results. In a single-blind study only the participants are kept unaware, usually by giving the control group a placebo that looks just like the real treatment. This controls the placebo effect (improving because you believe you are being treated) and demand characteristics, the cues that tell participants what the researcher hopes to find (Martin Orne, 1962). In a double-blind study neither the participants nor the people who give the treatment and record the outcomes know who is in which group, which also controls the experimenter expectancy effects shown by Robert Rosenthal (1966). A triple-blind study also blinds those who analyse the data. Example: Riya tests whether a herbal tea improves sleep; half her participants get the tea and half an identical-tasting placebo tea, and none of them knows which they received, so their sleep reports cannot be swayed by belief alone. Option d is right because keeping participants unaware of their group is the defining feature of a blind (single-blind) experiment. Exam trap: option c describes a double-blind experiment, the stronger design that also blinds the experimenters; it is a blind design too, but 'blind experiment' on its own refers to the basic case in which the participants are unaware. Key note: a double-blind study (option c) is also a kind of blind experiment, so c is arguable; but the textbook definition of a 'blind' experiment, with no 'double', is the single-blind case in which only the participants are kept unaware, so the answer used here, d, stands.", "topic": "Single-blind experiments", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is true?", "options": {"a": "Interactions can occur without there being a main effect of any independent variable", "b": "If two independent variables each have a main effect on the dependent variable, then an interaction will occur", "c": "Interactions can only occur when at least one independent variable has an individual effect on the dependent variable", "d": "None of the answers is correct"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: an interaction can occur even when neither independent variable has a main effect. In a factorial design, set out by Ronald Fisher (1935) and analysed with factorial ANOVA, two or more independent variables (IVs) are crossed, so every level of one is combined with every level of the other. A main effect is the effect of one IV averaged over the levels of the other. An interaction exists when the effect of one IV depends on the level of the other; on a graph the lines are not parallel. Main effects and interactions are tested separately and are independent of one another, so any combination can occur. Example: Kabir compares two teaching methods with visual and verbal learners. Method A gives visual learners an average of 80 and verbal learners 60; Method B gives visual learners 60 and verbal learners 80. Each method averages 70 and each type of learner averages 70, so neither IV has a main effect, yet the lines cross completely: the better method depends on the learner. This is a crossover (disordinal) interaction. Option a is right because main effects are averages, and opposite effects at different levels can cancel out in the average while the interaction stays large. Exam trap: students assume an interaction needs at least one main effect, or that two main effects must produce an interaction; neither is true, because the two main effects and the interaction in a two-way design are separate effects.", "topic": "Interactions and main effects", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Measures of central tendency are:", "options": {"a": "Inferential statistics that identify the best single value for representing a set of data", "b": "Descriptive statistics that identify the best single value for representing a set of data", "c": "Inferential statistics that identify the spread of scores in a data set", "d": "Descriptive statistics that identify the spread of scores in a data set"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: measures of central tendency are descriptive statistics that identify the best single value for representing a set of data. Descriptive statistics summarise the data you actually have; inferential statistics (the t test, the F test, chi-square) use a sample to draw conclusions about a population. A measure of central tendency is one score that stands for the whole distribution, its typical or central value. There are three main ones. The mean is the arithmetic average (the sum of the scores divided by N); it uses every score but is pulled by extreme scores. The median is the middle score when the scores are put in order, the 50th percentile; it resists outliers, so it suits skewed data such as income, and ordinal data. The mode is the most frequent score and the only average for nominal data. Example: Meera's quiz marks are 4, 7, 7, 9 and 13: the mean is 8, the median 7 and the mode 7. Option b is right because these measures describe a data set with a single representative value and make no inference beyond it. Exam trap: two separate distinctions are tested at once, descriptive versus inferential and central tendency versus variability; spread (range, variance, standard deviation) is variability, not central tendency, and both kinds of measure are descriptive.", "topic": "Measures of central tendency", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a characteristic of the mean?", "options": {"a": "It is affected by extreme scores", "b": "It minimizes the sum of squared deviations", "c": "The sum of the deviations about the mean is zero", "d": "It is best used with the ordinal data"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: 'it is best used with ordinal data' is not a characteristic of the mean. The arithmetic mean (M = ΣX ÷ N) is the sum of the scores divided by their number. It has three classic properties. First, it is the balance point of the distribution: deviations above and below it cancel, so the sum of deviations about the mean is always zero. Second, the sum of squared deviations about the mean is smaller than about any other value, the least-squares property that makes the mean the basis of variance, SD and regression. Third, because it uses every score, it is affected by extreme scores: the marks 4, 7, 7, 9 and 13 have a mean of 8, but adding one score of 40 lifts it to about 13.3. The mean assumes equal distances between scores, so it needs interval or ratio data. Ordinal data, such as class ranks or finishing positions in a race, show only order; the gap between first and second need not equal the gap between second and third, so the median is the proper average, and for nominal data the mode. Example: if Arjun's teacher records only class ranks, the median rank is a fair summary, but a mean rank assumes that the gap between 1st and 2nd equals the gap between 9th and 10th, which ranks cannot tell us. Option d is right because ordinal data suit the median, not the mean. Exam trap: the item asks for what is NOT true; options a, b and c are all genuine properties, so a hurried reader picks the first true statement.", "topic": "Properties of the mean", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The sum of squared deviations is a good measure of variability except:", "options": {"a": "It uses the mean in all its calculations", "b": "It can only be used with ratio data", "c": "It does not take N into account", "d": "It cannot be used in any other statistical analysis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the sum of squared deviations is a poor measure of variability on its own because it does not take N, the number of scores, into account. The sum of squares (SS) is found by taking each score's deviation from the mean, squaring it and adding up the squares: SS = the sum of (X minus the mean) squared. Squaring removes negative signs and gives extra weight to large deviations. SS is the building block of the variance, the standard deviation, ANOVA and regression. Its flaw is that it keeps growing as scores are added, even when the spread stays the same, so SS values from groups of different sizes cannot be compared. Dividing SS by N (or by N minus 1 for a sample estimate of the population) gives the variance, an average squared deviation, and the square root of the variance gives the standard deviation, back in the original units. Example: Arjun's group of 5 students and Sana's group of 50 have marks spread equally widely around their means, yet Sana's SS is roughly ten times larger simply because she has ten times as many scores. Option c is right because SS ignores the number of scores and so cannot by itself compare variability across groups. Exam trap: option a sounds like a criticism, but measuring deviations from the mean is exactly what makes SS work (the sum of squared deviations about the mean is the smallest possible), so it is a strength, not a flaw.", "topic": "Sum of squares and N", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following descriptive statistical procedures cannot be used with a continuous variable?", "options": {"a": "A frequency distribution", "b": "A grouped frequency distribution", "c": "A median", "d": "A mean"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: a simple (ungrouped) frequency distribution is the procedure that cannot sensibly be used with a continuous variable. A frequency distribution lists every score and how many people obtained it. That works for a discrete variable, which takes only separate values (number of children, number of errors, a 1 to 5 rating), because the scores repeat and the table stays short. A continuous variable, such as reaction time, height or weight, can take any value in a range, limited only by the precision of measurement, so almost every score is different (0.512, 0.518, 0.523 seconds and so on). A table of every value with a frequency of 1 beside each would be as long as the raw data and show no pattern. Continuous data are therefore summarised in a grouped frequency distribution, with scores pooled into equal class intervals, or drawn as a histogram or frequency polygon. The median and the mean work well with continuous data, which are usually interval or ratio. Example: Ishaan records the reaction times of 60 students to the nearest millisecond and finds 58 different values, so he groups them into ten intervals to see the shape. Option a is right because a simple frequency distribution suits discrete scores, while continuous scores need grouping. Exam trap: option b, the grouped frequency distribution, is exactly the tool designed for continuous data, so do not let the shared words 'frequency distribution' pull you towards it. Key note: once continuous scores are rounded you can technically tally them in a simple frequency table, but the textbook point is that a simple frequency distribution suits discrete data and continuous data need grouping, so the answer used here, a, stands.", "topic": "Frequency distributions for continuous data", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Purposive samples contain participants who:", "options": {"a": "Meet the criteria of being available to the researcher", "b": "Meet specific purposes or criteria related to the aims of the study", "c": "Are in the study field when something unexpected occurs during data collection", "d": "Represent each group within the accessible sample"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: purposive samples contain participants who meet specific purposes or criteria related to the aims of the study. Purposive sampling, also called judgemental or purposeful sampling, is a non-probability method: there is no random selection, so the chance of any person being chosen is unknown. Instead the researcher uses knowledge of the topic to hand-pick people who fit criteria set by the research question, such as typical cases, experts, key informants or information-rich cases. It is common in qualitative research, where the aim is depth of understanding rather than generalisation to a population; Michael Quinn Patton (1990) set out many purposeful strategies, such as typical case, extreme case, maximum variation and criterion sampling. Its weakness is bias, since the sample depends on the researcher's judgement, and results cannot be generalised statistically. Example: to study the stress of caring for a child with autism, Dr Lakshmi recruits only mothers whose children have a confirmed autism diagnosis and who have been the main carer for at least two years. Option b is right because the defining feature of purposive sampling is that people are chosen deliberately against criteria tied to the study's purpose. Exam trap: availability alone (option a) is convenience sampling, and representing each group in fixed numbers (option d) is quota sampling; both are also non-probability methods, but neither selects people for fitting the study's aims.", "topic": "Purposive sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If we want to quantify the degree of correlation between academic achievement and creativity, which correlation coefficient shall we use?", "options": {"a": "Pearson Product Moment correlation", "b": "Spearman's rank order correlation", "c": "Either would be appropriate", "d": "It would depend on how the variables would be measured"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the choice of coefficient depends on how academic achievement and creativity are measured. Correlation coefficients are matched to the level of measurement and the shape of the data. Karl Pearson's (1896) product-moment correlation, r, suits two variables measured on interval or ratio scales whose relationship is linear and whose distributions are roughly normal, without extreme outliers. Charles Spearman's (1904) rank-order correlation, rho, correlates ranks: it suits ordinal data, monotonic but non-linear relationships, and skewed data with outliers, since ranking tames extreme scores. Neither variable in the stem has a fixed level of measurement. Academic achievement could be percentage marks (treated as interval) or class rank (ordinal); creativity could be a score on E. Paul Torrance's Tests of Creative Thinking (1966) or a teacher's ranking of the most creative pupils. Example: if Nikhil has each student's exam percentage and Torrance score and the scatter plot looks linear, he uses Pearson's r; if the teacher has only ranked the students on creativity, he uses Spearman's rho. Option d is right because the right statistic cannot be chosen until the scale of measurement of both variables is known. Exam trap: option c sounds broad-minded, but the two coefficients are not interchangeable; Pearson's r on ranks or badly skewed data distorts the result, and Spearman's rho on good interval data throws information away.", "topic": "Choosing Pearson or Spearman", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who introduced the theory of measurement and scales of measurement in Psychology?", "options": {"a": "Stevens", "b": "Schlosberg", "c": "Stanley", "d": "Skinner"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) introduced the theory of measurement and the scales of measurement in psychology. Stevens, a Harvard psychophysicist, published 'On the theory of scales of measurement' in Science in 1946. He defined measurement as the assignment of numerals to objects or events according to rules, and argued that different rules create different kinds of scale. He named four levels, each adding a property: nominal (categories only, such as gender or blood group), ordinal (rank order, such as class position), interval (equal units but an arbitrary zero, such as Celsius temperature or IQ) and ratio (equal units plus a true zero, such as reaction time or weight). He also set out which statistics are permissible at each level: the mode for nominal, the median and percentiles for ordinal, the mean and standard deviation for interval, and meaningful ratios of scores only for ratio scales. The memory hook is NOIR. Stevens is also known for his power law of psychophysics (1957): perceived magnitude grows as a power of stimulus intensity. Example: Tara's survey records her classmates' religion (nominal), their rank in class (ordinal), their IQ (interval) and their monthly pocket money (ratio). Option a is right because the four-level theory of measurement scales is Stevens's best-known contribution. Exam trap: all four options are psychologists whose surnames begin with S, so the item rests on recall; Stanley (option c) is a tempting decoy because Stevens's own first name is Stanley, but Julian Stanley is known for research designs.", "topic": "Stevens's scales of measurement", "fig": "Stanley Smith Stevens"}, {"q": "What sort of data is income?", "options": {"a": "Interval", "b": "Nominal", "c": "Ordinal", "d": "Ratio"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: income is ratio data. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels: nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio. A ratio scale has all the properties of the lower levels (categories, order and equal intervals) plus a true zero, a zero that means none of the quantity. Income has every one of these. Zero rupees means no income at all; each rupee is the same amount wherever it falls on the scale; and ratios make sense, so Rs 60,000 a month is twice Rs 30,000. Height, weight, age and reaction time are other ratio variables. An interval scale also has equal units but an arbitrary zero: 0 degrees Celsius is not the absence of temperature, so 40 degrees is not twice as hot as 20. Example: Rahul earns Rs 45,000 a month and his sister Pooja Rs 90,000; saying Pooja earns twice as much is meaningful, which only a ratio scale allows. Option d is right because income has equal units and an absolute zero. Exam trap: income recorded in brackets such as low, middle and high becomes ordinal data; as a raw amount of money, though, it is ratio.", "topic": "Income as ratio data", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Norms is a term used in psychometrics to refer to the test performance data of:", "options": {"a": "People tested at a different time than another group of test takers", "b": "Test takers who constitute a control group in an experiment", "c": "A particular group of test takers to be used for comparison", "d": "A sample of people with no prior training in the tested area"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: in psychometrics, norms are the test performance data of a particular group of test takers, designed to be used as a reference for comparison when evaluating or interpreting individual scores. This is the definition used by Ronald Jay Cohen and Mark Swerdlik in their textbook Psychological Testing and Assessment. A raw score on its own means little: 34 out of 50 could be excellent or poor. To interpret it, the test developer gives the test under standard conditions to a large normative (standardisation) sample, chosen to represent the people the test is meant for by age, sex, region and so on. Their scores become the norms, usually set out as percentile ranks, standard scores or age and grade equivalents, and a person's score is placed relative to this group: norm-referenced interpretation. Common types are age, grade, national, local and subgroup norms; good norms are large, representative, recent and relevant to the person tested. Example: Aditi, aged 10, scores 34 on a reading test; the manual's norms show this is at the 80th percentile for Indian 10-year-olds, so she reads better than 80 percent of her age group. Option c is right because norms exist precisely to serve as a reference group for comparison. Exam trap: norms describe how people actually perform, not how they should perform, so they are not standards; and norm-referenced interpretation (compared with other people) differs from criterion-referenced interpretation (compared with a fixed standard of mastery).", "topic": "Test norms", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to the inter rater reliability?", "options": {"a": "The degree to which two tests measure the same construct", "b": "The degree to which a clinician can predict future behavior", "c": "The degree to which the two clinicians will agree on interpretation or scoring of a test", "d": "The degree to which the items in the test relate to each other"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: inter-rater reliability is the degree to which two clinicians will agree on the interpretation or scoring of a test. Reliability is consistency of measurement, and it has several forms. Inter-rater (inter-scorer or inter-observer) reliability asks whether two or more people who independently score, rate or interpret the same responses reach the same result. High agreement means the scoring is objective rather than dependent on who did it. It matters most wherever judgement enters: projective tests such as the Rorschach and the TAT, essay marking, behavioural observation and psychiatric diagnosis. Simple percentage agreement flatters raters, because some agreement happens by chance; Jacob Cohen's (1960) kappa corrects for chance agreement in categorical judgements, and the intraclass correlation is used for ratings on a scale. Example: two psychologists, Anil and Deepa, independently score the same 20 Rorschach records; if they code the same determinants for nearly every response, inter-rater reliability is high. Option c is right because agreement between independent scorers on the same test is exactly what inter-rater reliability means. Exam trap: the distractors are other psychometric terms in disguise; two tests measuring the same construct is convergent validity, predicting future behaviour is predictive validity, and items relating to each other is internal consistency.", "topic": "Inter-rater reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is assessed by Cronbach's alpha?", "options": {"a": "Concurrent validity", "b": "Inter rater validity", "c": "Test retest validity", "d": "Internal consistency"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: Cronbach's alpha assesses internal consistency. Lee Cronbach (1951) introduced coefficient alpha to estimate how far the items of a test hang together, that is, how consistently they measure the same underlying construct. Alpha rises when the items correlate with one another and when there are more items. Conceptually it is the mean of all possible split-half coefficients, so it avoids the arbitrary choice of a single split. It generalises the KR-20 formula of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937), which works only for items scored right or wrong, to items with several score points, such as Likert ratings. About 0.70 is the usual minimum (Jum Nunnally, 1978). Example: Sneha builds a 10-item exam-anxiety scale; if students who agree with one item tend to agree with the others, alpha is high. Option d is right because alpha is the standard index of internal consistency reliability. Exam trap: the distractors are mislabelled; 'inter rater validity' and 'test retest validity' are really reliabilities (agreement between scorers and stability over time), and concurrent validity is a criterion validity checked against an outside measure taken at the same time.", "topic": "Cronbach's alpha", "fig": "Lee Cronbach"}, {"q": "The process of developing a test occurs in 5 stages beginning with test conceptualization. What is the 5th stage of this process?", "options": {"a": "Item analysis", "b": "Test revision", "c": "Test tryout", "d": "Test construction"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: test revision is the fifth and final stage of test development. Ronald Jay Cohen and Mark Swerdlik (Psychological Testing and Assessment, 7th edition, 2010) describe five stages. (1) Test conceptualisation: the developer decides what the test will measure, for whom and why, often after some pilot work. (2) Test construction: items are written, a scaling method is chosen (for example a Likert-type or Guttman scale) and a scoring plan is fixed. (3) Test tryout: the draft is given to a sample of people like the intended test takers, under the same conditions as the final test. (4) Item analysis: the tryout data are used to judge each item by its difficulty, its discrimination (whether high scorers pass it more often than low scorers) and, for multiple-choice items, its distractors. (5) Test revision: weak items are dropped or rewritten and the revised test is tried out again, so the last stages can loop until the test works. Example: Meera builds an exam-anxiety scale; tryout and item analysis show that six items fail to discriminate, so she rewrites them and tries the scale again. Option b is right because revision is the stage that acts on everything learned in the earlier four. Exam trap: item analysis is the fourth stage, not the fifth; and Indian texts often list longer sequences ending with norms and the manual, but five stages beginning with conceptualisation is the Cohen and Swerdlik scheme.", "topic": "Stages of test development", "fig": "Ronald Jay Cohen and Mark Swerdlik"}, {"q": "In the context of Rorschach scoring, which does not belong?", "options": {"a": "Form", "b": "Concepts", "c": "Determinants", "d": "Popularity"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: 'concepts' is not a Rorschach scoring category, whereas form, determinants and popularity all belong to Rorschach scoring. Hermann Rorschach (1921, Psychodiagnostik) built a test of ten inkblots and asked people what each might be. Later scoring systems, chiefly those of Samuel Beck and Bruno Klopfer in the 1930s and John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974), which drew on them, code every response on the same main categories. Location: which part of the blot was used (W whole, D common detail, Dd unusual detail, S white space). Determinants: what feature of the blot shaped the percept: form (F), movement (M for human, FM for animal), colour (C) and shading. Content: what was seen (H human, A animal, anatomy, nature and so on). Popularity: whether the answer is a popular (P) response, given very often by the normative group, or an original one. Form quality: how well the percept fits the blot's real shape, the main index of reality testing. Example: Arjun looks at card V and says 'a bat, the whole thing, because of its shape'. That is coded W (location), F (determinant), A (content) and P (popular). Option b is right because what the person sees is coded as content; there is no category called concepts. Exam trap: 'concepts' is a near miss for 'content'; and 'form' can look like the odd one out because it is not a heading of its own, but form is the commonest determinant and form quality is scored in every major system.", "topic": "Rorschach scoring categories", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "In clinical practice, a TAT examiner administers:", "options": {"a": "At least 3 cards", "b": "10 cards", "c": "20 cards", "d": "As many as he or she cares to administer"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: in clinical practice a TAT examiner gives as many cards as he or she judges useful; there is no fixed number. The Thematic Apperception Test was introduced by Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935) at the Harvard Psychological Clinic. The 1943 set has 31 cards: 30 pictures of people in ambiguous scenes plus one blank card (card 16), with some cards marked for boys, girls, men or women. For each card the person tells a story: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters think and feel, and how it ends. Murray read the stories for the hero's needs (such as achievement and affiliation) and the press of the environment. His manual planned 20 cards in two sessions of 10, the second set more unusual and dramatic. Clinicians rarely follow this plan. They choose a smaller set whose themes suit the referral question, often around 8 to 12 cards and sometimes fewer, and the choice differs from one examiner to the next. Example: a psychologist assessing Kabir, a teenager in conflict with his father, picks card 1 (a boy looking at a violin, which pulls for achievement themes) and cards showing an older and a younger man. Option d is right because the number and choice of cards are left to the examiner's clinical judgement. Exam trap: 20 cards is Murray's original plan and the number most students remember, so it lures recall; 10 is just one of his two sessions.", "topic": "TAT administration", "fig": "Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray"}, {"q": "In administering the Rorschach, the inquiry refers to that part of the examination in which:", "options": {"a": "The examiner obtains complete demographic information", "b": "The examiner exposes the cards to the test taker for the first time", "c": "The examiner exposes the cards to the test taker for the second time", "d": "The test taker is permitted to ask the examiner any question at all"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the Rorschach, the inquiry is the part of the examination in which the cards are shown to the test taker for the second time. Hermann Rorschach (1921) published ten inkblots: five achromatic, two black and red, and three multicoloured. In the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) administration has two main phases. In the response phase (free association) the examiner hands over the cards one at a time in a fixed order, asks 'What might this be?', and records every response word for word without suggesting answers. In the inquiry phase, after all ten cards, the examiner goes back through each card and each response to learn where on the blot it was seen (location), what made it look that way, such as form, colour, shading or movement (determinants), and what it was (content), using non-leading questions such as 'What made it look like that?'. An optional testing-the-limits phase may follow, in which the examiner asks directly about popular responses or determinants the person never used. Option c is right because the inquiry depends on re-exposing the same cards so that each response can be scored. For example, if Riya said 'a bat' to Card I, in the inquiry the examiner hands her Card I again and asks her to show where the bat is and what made it look like one. Exam trap: option b, the first exposure, is the response phase; the inquiry always comes after it and never uses leading questions.", "topic": "Rorschach inquiry phase", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "The difference between objective and projective methods of assessment is that projective methods are:", "options": {"a": "More artistic and less verbal in nature", "b": "Of value with test takers regardless of culture", "c": "Indirect methods of personality assessment", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: projective methods are indirect ways of assessing personality, whereas objective tests are direct. An objective (structured) test such as the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) asks clear questions about the person ('I often feel tense': true or false) and scores fixed answers with a scoring key. The person describes himself or herself, so the method is direct. Lawrence Frank (1939) coined the term projective methods for the reverse approach. The stimulus is ambiguous (an inkblot, a vague picture, half a sentence), instructions are minimal, almost any answer is accepted and the purpose is disguised. Because the stimulus has so little structure, whatever structure the person gives it is assumed to come from inside: the projective hypothesis. The examiner never asks about personality; it is inferred from how the person responds. The disguise is also the claimed advantage, since people cannot easily fake answers when they do not know what a 'good' answer looks like. Example: asked 'Do you worry about failing?', Riya can simply say no; asked to tell a story about a TAT card, she describes a girl who studies all night and still fails, and the examiner infers the worry. Option c is right because indirectness is the defining difference between the two approaches. Exam trap: 'All of the above' tempts because some projective tests use drawings and pictures can look culture-free, but most projective responses are verbal and the tests are strongly culture-bound.", "topic": "Projective versus objective tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In family therapy, the family therapist will meet:", "options": {"a": "Separately with the members of the client's family to ask them to describe the family dynamics from their own perspective", "b": "With all the members of the client's family to analyze the way in which individuals interact", "c": "With the client and individual members of their family in pairs to analyze the way in which the two interact", "d": "With the clients and encourage them to engage in role play to represent the behavior of the family members"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: in family therapy the therapist usually meets all the members of the client's family together to analyse and change the way they interact. Family therapy, which took shape in the 1950s, rests on general systems theory (Ludwig von Bertalanffy, whose ideas are summed up in his book General System Theory, 1968): a family is a system whose parts affect one another, so a change in one member changes the whole. The person brought for help is the 'identified patient', the visible sign of a troubled system rather than its cause, and problems are kept going by circular patterns, not by one person's fault. To see those patterns the therapist holds conjoint sessions with the whole family in the room and watches who speaks for whom, who interrupts, who sides with whom and what happens when conflict starts. Virginia Satir (Conjoint Family Therapy, 1964) made such joint sessions central, and Salvador Minuchin (1974) asked families to act out their conflicts in the session (enactment). Example: fourteen-year-old Arjun is skipping school. In a joint session with his parents and sister, the therapist notices that his mother answers every question put to him while his father stays silent, a pattern that no single interview would reveal. Option b is right because analysing interaction among all the members together is the core of the method. Exam trap: some family therapists also see individuals (Murray Bowen, 1978, often coached one motivated member) or use role play and family sculpting, but the defining format is the whole family meeting together.", "topic": "Family therapy format", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The client's right to agree to participate in counseling, assessment, or other professional activities or services, after such services are explained and understood is:", "options": {"a": "Professional responsibility", "b": "Personal responsibility", "c": "Nonprofessional responsibility", "d": "Informed consent"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: informed consent is the client's right to agree to, or refuse, counselling, assessment or any other professional service after it has been explained and understood. It has four parts. Disclosure: the professional explains the purpose and nature of the service, likely risks and benefits, alternatives, fees, the limits of confidentiality (for example, a duty to act if someone is at risk of serious harm) and the right to withdraw at any time. Understanding: the explanation is given in language the client can follow. Capacity: the person is able to decide; for a child, a parent or guardian gives permission and the child gives assent. Voluntariness: agreement is free of pressure. Consent is a continuing process, revisited whenever the service changes. The American Counseling Association's Code of Ethics (2014) covers it in section A.2, and the American Psychological Association's Ethics Code (2002, amended 2010 and 2016) in Standards 3.10, 9.03 (assessment) and 10.01 (therapy). Example: before Meera's first session, her counsellor explains how sessions work, the fee, what would have to be shared with others and that she may stop whenever she likes; Meera then signs. Option d is right because the stem is the textbook definition of informed consent. Exam trap: the other options are vague 'responsibility' words; professional responsibility is the practitioner's duty, not the client's right, and informed consent should not be confused with confidentiality, which protects what the client discloses.", "topic": "Informed consent", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following interventions is based on the view that the childhood problems result from inappropriate family structure and organization?", "options": {"a": "Systemic family therapy", "b": "Parent learning programmes", "c": "Functional family therapy", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: systemic family therapy, in its structural form, holds that children's problems come from an inappropriate family structure and organisation. Salvador Minuchin (Families and Family Therapy, 1974) described every family as having a structure: subsystems (spousal, parental, sibling), a hierarchy with the parents in charge, and boundaries that regulate closeness. Healthy boundaries are clear, firm yet permeable. Diffuse boundaries produce enmeshment (over-involvement, little autonomy); rigid boundaries produce disengagement (isolation, little support). Trouble also comes from cross-generational coalitions, such as a mother and son allied against the father. The child's symptom is read as a product of this faulty organisation, so the therapist joins the family, maps its structure, has members enact their conflicts in the session and then restructures the family by strengthening the parental subsystem and redrawing boundaries. Structural therapy is one of the systemic family therapies, which all treat the family as a system. Example: ten-year-old Kabir refuses school; the therapist sees that he sleeps in his mother's room while his father has been pushed out, and works to put both parents back in charge together. Option a is right because structure and organisation are the core ideas of the structural, systemic approach. Exam trap: parent training and functional family therapy are real family interventions, so 'All of the above' tempts, but neither blames faulty structure. Key note: functional family therapy also takes a systems view, and some texts would class it as systemic; but its focus is the function a behaviour serves, while the words 'structure and organisation' point to Minuchin's structural school under the systemic heading, and the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Structural family therapy", "fig": "Salvador Minuchin"}, {"q": "Which type of Psychotherapy views the process of helping others as a form of teaching?", "options": {"a": "Humanistic therapy", "b": "Behavior therapy", "c": "Psychotherapy", "d": "Client centered therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: behaviour therapy views helping others as a form of teaching. It applies experimentally established principles of learning to psychological problems, building on Joseph Wolpe (1958), Hans Eysenck (1959) and the operant work of B. F. Skinner (1953). Its starting assumption is that problem behaviour is learned by the same laws as normal behaviour, so it can be unlearned and better behaviour learned in its place. Therapy is therefore new learning, and the therapist acts as a teacher or coach: defining the target behaviour, explaining the plan, teaching skills step by step (relaxation, assertiveness, social and problem-solving skills), modelling them (Albert Bandura's Principles of Behavior Modification, 1969, made modelling a core technique), having the client rehearse, giving feedback, setting homework and reinforcing progress. The aim is for clients to become their own therapists through self-management. Example: shy Riya cannot refuse her classmates' requests. The therapist models a polite refusal, Riya role-plays it, gets feedback and practises it with her roommate as homework. Option b is right because behaviour therapy openly casts the therapist as an instructor of new behaviour. Exam trap: client-centred therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) is itself a humanistic therapy, so a and d share the same non-directive stance in which the therapist facilitates growth and the client leads, the opposite of teaching; and 'psychotherapy' is the umbrella term, not a type.", "topic": "Behaviour therapist as teacher", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Is it possible to skip a few steps in systematic desensitization or is it always necessary to go through each step in the hierarchy?", "options": {"a": "It is possible to skip steps if you are making rapid progress", "b": "Skipping is not recommended because anxiety may overwhelm the individual", "c": "It is advised that people skip steps so that they do not become bored", "d": "Early steps can be skipped not the later ones"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: skipping steps is not recommended, because the jump may bring more anxiety than the client can handle. Joseph Wolpe (1958) built systematic desensitisation on reciprocal inhibition: two incompatible responses, such as deep relaxation and anxiety, cannot occur together. The method has three parts. First the client learns progressive muscle relaxation, from Edmund Jacobson (1929; revised 1938). Next client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy of about 10 to 20 situations, each rated on Wolpe's Subjective Units of Disturbance Scale (SUDS, also read as subjective units of distress) from 0 (calm) to 100 (worst imaginable). Then, fully relaxed, the client imagines the lowest item. If anxiety appears, the client raises a finger, stops imagining and relaxes again; the item is repeated until it no longer causes anxiety, and only then does the client move up one step. Each step is small so that relaxation can outweigh the anxiety it brings. Skipping steps makes the gap too big: anxiety overwhelms the relaxation, the scene is paired with fear again, and the fear may grow stronger or the client drop out. If a gap proves too large, the therapist adds an in-between item instead of leaping. Example: Arjun, afraid of dogs, climbs from a photo of a puppy to a video of dogs, then a dog barking nearby. Option b is right because graded, step-by-step progress is the heart of the method. Exam trap: d sounds sensible because early items are easy, but the easy items are where the calm response is first built, so no part of the hierarchy is skipped.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation hierarchy", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "Career counseling evolved as a result of:", "options": {"a": "The need to understand the world of work and how to best prepare individuals to enter it", "b": "The need to find a suitable match between the demands of the workplace and the needs of the worker", "c": "The need to find a suitable match between the skills and the needs of the individuals within the workplace", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: career counselling evolved from all three needs, which together make up its founding idea. Vocational guidance began in the United States in the early 1900s, when industrialisation, city growth and immigration created many new kinds of jobs and many young people with no idea how to choose among them. Frank Parsons opened the Vocation Bureau of Boston in 1908, and his book Choosing a Vocation (1909) set out three steps: a clear understanding of yourself (aptitudes, abilities, interests, resources and limits); knowledge of the requirements and conditions of success in different lines of work; and 'true reasoning' about how the two fit. This became the trait-and-factor approach. Option a is the second step, learning about the world of work and preparing to enter it. Option b is the matching of the workplace's demands with what the worker needs. Option c is the matching of an individual's skills and needs with a place in the workplace. Later theories refined these strands: John Holland (1959; 1973) matched six personality types with six work environments (RIASEC), and Donald Super (1953) treated career choice as development across the life span. Example: Meera, a commerce graduate, takes an interest inventory, reads about jobs in finance and teaching, and with her counsellor reasons out which fits her best. Option d is right because each listed need is a genuine part of how career counselling evolved. Exam trap: each single option is true, so choosing any one alone gives an incomplete answer.", "topic": "Origins of career counselling", "fig": "Frank Parsons"}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques is a client centered therapist most likely to use?", "options": {"a": "Analysis", "b": "Giving advice", "c": "Reflection", "d": "Aversive conditioning"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: a client-centred therapist is most likely to use reflection. Carl Rogers (1942, 1951) built client-centred, later person-centred, therapy on the belief that every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive to grow, and that distress arises when the self-concept clashes with experience (incongruence). The therapist therefore does not diagnose, interpret or instruct. Instead, Rogers (1957) listed six necessary and sufficient conditions for change (including psychological contact and a client in incongruence); the therapist's three, the core conditions that let clients heal themselves, are: empathy (sensing the client's inner world as if it were one's own), unconditional positive regard (accepting the client without judgement) and congruence or genuineness (being real, not hiding behind a professional role). Reflection is the technique that shows empathy in action. The therapist restates the feeling behind the client's words, so the client feels understood and explores further. Example: Kabir says, 'My father keeps comparing me with my cousin.' The therapist replies, 'You sound hurt, and maybe tired of never being enough for him.' No advice, no theory; the direction stays with Kabir. This is why Rogers first called the approach non-directive. Option c is right because reflection of feeling is the signature, non-directive skill of the client-centred method. Exam trap: examiners surround reflection with directive look-alikes. Analysis and interpretation belong to Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, giving advice to directive counselling, and aversive conditioning to behaviour therapy; only reflection keeps the locus of evaluation inside the client.", "topic": "Client-centred reflection", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "Counselling is a profession that aims to:", "options": {"a": "Promote personal growth and productivity", "b": "Provide a successful diagnosis in psychotherapy", "c": "Ensure that clients are on the right medication", "d": "Solely address behavior"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: counselling aims to promote personal growth and productivity. Counselling is a professional helping relationship, usually with fairly well-functioning people facing developmental, educational, career, relationship or situational problems. It works mainly in the present, is often brief, and aims at growth, wellness, prevention and effective functioning, rather than at treating severe disorder, which is the main business of psychotherapy and psychiatry. The American Counseling Association's consensus definition (2010) reads: 'Counseling is a professional relationship that empowers diverse individuals, families, and groups to accomplish mental health, wellness, education, and career goals.' Goals commonly listed in textbooks are helping clients change behaviour, cope better, make decisions, improve relationships and develop their potential. Carl Rogers (1942) used the words counselling and psychotherapy almost interchangeably, but most modern texts keep counselling for the growth end of the range. Example: Kabir, a final-year student, feels stuck and anxious about his future. His counsellor helps him see his strengths, set goals and plan his study, so that he works better and feels more in charge; no diagnosis or medicine is involved. Option a is right because growth and effective, productive living are what counselling sets out to achieve. Exam trap: absolute words such as 'solely' and 'ensure' usually mark wrong options, and diagnosis and medication belong to clinical psychology and psychiatry, not to the aims of counselling.", "topic": "Aims of counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Sensory _____ is the tendency of the sensory receptors to respond less and less to a constant stimulus.", "options": {"a": "Threshold", "b": "Adaptation", "c": "Perception", "d": "Spectrum"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: sensory adaptation is the tendency of sensory receptors to respond less and less to a constant, unchanging stimulus. It happens at the level of the receptors: when stimulation stays the same they fire less, so the sensation fades. This is useful, because it frees attention for changes in the environment, which usually matter more than things that stay the same. You stop smelling your own house, stop feeling the watch on your wrist and find a cold pool comfortable after a few minutes. Vision seems not to adapt only because the eyes are never perfectly still: tiny involuntary movements keep shifting the image onto fresh receptors. When an image is held at the same spot on the retina with special apparatus, it fades within seconds (stabilised retinal image studies by Lorrin Riggs and colleagues, 1953, and Roy Pritchard, 1961). Example: Meera walks into her aunt's kitchen and notices the strong smell of hing; ten minutes later she cannot smell it at all, though nothing has changed. Option b is right because a fading response to a steady stimulus is the definition of adaptation. Exam trap: adaptation is not habituation, a learned decrease in the brain's response to a repeated stimulus (you can still hear the ticking clock if you attend to it); and a threshold, the minimum intensity that can be detected (measured by Gustav Fechner, 1860), is a level, not a process. Dark adaptation is a different case, a rise in sensitivity in dim light.", "topic": "Sensory adaptation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The unconscious perception of stimuli that are too weak to exceed the absolute threshold for detection is known as:", "options": {"a": "Just noticeable difference", "b": "Difference threshold", "c": "Subliminal perception", "d": "Response bias"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: subliminal perception is the unconscious perception of stimuli too weak or brief to exceed the absolute threshold for detection. 'Limen' is Latin for threshold, so subliminal means below the threshold. The absolute threshold is the weakest stimulus a person detects 50 per cent of the time (Gustav Fechner, 1860). A subliminal stimulus is not consciously reported, yet it can still be processed. The best evidence comes from masked priming: Anthony Marcel (1983) flashed words so briefly, followed by a pattern mask, that people could not say what they had seen, yet an unseen word still speeded their responses to a related word. Such effects are real but small and short-lived. The famous claim of James Vicary (1957) that flashing 'Eat popcorn' and 'Drink Coca-Cola' during a film raised cinema sales was a hoax that he admitted in 1962, and there is no good evidence that subliminal advertising changes what people buy. Example: Riya sees a smiling face for a few milliseconds, instantly masked; she reports seeing nothing, but then rates an unfamiliar Chinese character a little more favourably. Option c is right because the stem defines subliminal perception exactly. Exam trap: a and b are one and the same thing, the just noticeable difference or difference threshold (Ernst Weber, 1834), which concerns noticing a change between two stimuli, not perceiving a stimulus below the absolute threshold.", "topic": "Subliminal perception", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The brightness of visual sensation is determined by the:", "options": {"a": "Intensity of the light wave", "b": "Frequency of the light wave", "c": "Saturation", "d": "Radiation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the brightness of a visual sensation is determined mainly by the intensity (amplitude) of the light wave. Light is electromagnetic energy, and the human eye responds to wavelengths of about 380 to 760 nanometres. A light wave has three physical properties, and each maps onto one psychological dimension of what we see. Intensity, the amount of energy carried (the height or amplitude of the wave), is experienced as brightness: the more intense the light, the brighter it looks. Wavelength, or its inverse, frequency, is experienced as hue, the colour itself (long waves look red, short waves violet). Purity, how narrow the mix of wavelengths is, is experienced as saturation, the vividness of the colour. Hearing follows the same pattern: amplitude gives loudness, frequency gives pitch and complexity gives timbre. The link is not one to one: brightness grows much more slowly than intensity (the power law of Stanley Smith Stevens, 1957, gives brightness an exponent of about 0.33), and the eye is more sensitive to some wavelengths than others, but intensity is the main determinant. Example: when Arjun dims his phone screen at night, the colours keep the same hue but everything looks darker, because less light energy reaches his eyes. Option a is right because intensity is the physical property that brightness tracks. Exam trap: frequency (wavelength) sets hue, not brightness, just as it sets pitch in hearing; and saturation is itself a dimension of colour experience, not its cause.", "topic": "Light intensity and brightness", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following describes the process of transduction?", "options": {"a": "When a friend plays a radio below your absolute threshold", "b": "When light waves are converted to neural impulses", "c": "When stimuli occur that are not attended to", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: transduction is the conversion of physical energy into neural impulses, as when light waves are converted into nerve signals in the eye. The brain cannot use light, sound waves or pressure directly; it works only with neural signals. Each sense therefore has receptor cells specialised for one kind of energy, and the conversion they perform is transduction, the first step of sensation. In the retina, rods and cones contain light-sensitive photopigments; when the rod pigment rhodopsin absorbs light it changes shape and starts a chemical chain that alters the cell's electrical activity, which other retinal cells pass on to the optic nerve. In the cochlea, sound vibrations bend hair cells; in the nose and tongue, chemical receptors respond to molecules; in the skin, mechanical receptors respond to pressure. After transduction come transmission to the brain and then perception, the brain's organisation and interpretation of the signals. Example: as Meera looks at a red rose, light reflected from the petals strikes her cones, which turn it into impulses that her brain then reads as a red rose. Option b is right because it describes exactly this energy-to-impulse conversion. Exam trap: transduction is neither transmission nor perception; and a radio played below your absolute threshold (a) is a subliminal stimulus, while stimuli that are not attended to (c) are a matter of attention, as in the inattentional blindness study of Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris (1999), not of conversion.", "topic": "Sensory transduction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "People who think that their ethnic, national, or religious group is superior to others are called:", "options": {"a": "Authoritarian", "b": "Dogmatic", "c": "Ethnocentric", "d": "Rigid"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: people who think that their own ethnic, national or religious group is superior to others are called ethnocentric. William Graham Sumner (Folkways, 1906) named the in-group ('we') and the out-group ('they') and defined ethnocentrism as the view of things in which one's own group is the centre of everything and all others are scaled and rated with reference to it. The ethnocentric person takes the customs, values and beliefs of the in-group as the natural standard, judges other groups by that standard and finds them strange, inferior or wrong. Some ethnocentrism appears in almost every culture and builds group loyalty, but strong ethnocentrism feeds stereotypes, prejudice and discrimination. Theodor Adorno and colleagues (The Authoritarian Personality, 1950) measured it with an E (ethnocentrism) scale and found that it went together with their F scale for authoritarianism, which is why the two are easily confused. Example: Kabir insists that his community's food, festivals and dress are the 'proper' ones and mocks a classmate's customs as backward; he is judging others by his own group's yardstick. Option c is right because believing one's own group superior is the very definition of ethnocentrism. Exam trap: authoritarian people are often ethnocentric, so a is the near miss, but authoritarianism is a whole personality type built around obedience to authority and rigid convention, while ethnocentrism is specifically the belief in one's own group's superiority.", "topic": "Ethnocentrism", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A real danger of groupthink is that it:", "options": {"a": "Is contagious", "b": "Occurs in cohesive groups", "c": "Disrupts coordinated efforts at group problem solving", "d": "Leads to a suspension of critical thinking"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: the real danger of groupthink is that it leads to a suspension of critical thinking, so the group makes poor decisions. Irving Janis (Victims of Groupthink, 1972; revised 1982) defined groupthink as a mode of thinking in a highly cohesive group in which the striving for unanimity overrides members' motivation to appraise alternatives realistically. Its antecedents are high cohesion, insulation from outsiders, a directive leader, high stress and no methodical procedure for weighing options. Janis listed eight symptoms: an illusion of invulnerability, collective rationalisation, belief in the group's inherent morality, stereotyped views of out-groups, direct pressure on dissenters, self-censorship, an illusion of unanimity and self-appointed mindguards who screen out unwelcome information. The result is defective decision making: few alternatives considered, risks unexamined, information ignored and no contingency plans. His classic case was President John F. Kennedy's advisers approving the Bay of Pigs invasion of Cuba (1961). Remedies include an impartial leader, a devil's advocate, outside experts and a second-chance meeting. Example: a college fest committee led by popular Arjun approves an open-air concert in the monsoon; doubters stay quiet to keep the harmony, and nobody plans for rain. Option d is right because suspended critical thinking is what turns a friendly group into a dangerous one. Exam trap: b is true, since cohesiveness is the main precondition, but it is a condition, not the danger; and c is reversed, because groupthink makes coordination smoother, not harder.", "topic": "Groupthink", "fig": "Irving Janis"}, {"q": "The chief function of the control group in an experiment is that it:", "options": {"a": "Allows mathematical relationship to be established", "b": "Provides a point of reference against which the behavior of the experimental group can be compared", "c": "Balances the experiment to eliminate all extraneous variables", "d": "Is not really necessary"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the chief function of the control group is to provide a point of reference against which the behaviour of the experimental group can be compared. In an experiment the researcher manipulates the independent variable (IV), holds other conditions constant and assigns participants to groups at random. The experimental group receives the treatment; the control group is treated in exactly the same way except that it does not receive the treatment, or receives a placebo. The control group therefore shows what would have happened anyway, through practice, maturation, the passage of time. If the two groups were equivalent at the start and differ on the dependent variable (DV) at the end, only the IV can explain the difference. This is the logic of John Stuart Mill's (1843) method of difference: when two cases differ in only one circumstance, that circumstance is the cause. Example: Meera wants to know whether a short mindfulness session lowers exam anxiety. She randomly assigns 40 students: 20 do the session and 20 listen to a neutral audio clip of the same length, and then all complete an anxiety scale. Without the control group, lower scores might simply reflect sitting quietly for a while. Option b is right because comparison with a baseline is what a control group is for. Exam trap: the control group does not eliminate extraneous variables (c); random assignment spreads them evenly across groups, and the control group lets their effects show up equally in both.", "topic": "Function of the control group", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-2016": [{"q": "The expression that \"you can't teach an old dog new tricks\" would support which theory of forgetting?", "concept": "The answer is c: the saying fits proactive interference, in which old learning gets in the way of new learning. Interference theory, championed by John McGeoch (1932), holds that we forget mainly because other memories compete with the one we want, not because traces simply fade with time. The competition can run in two directions. In proactive interference ('pro' means forward) earlier learning disrupts later learning: the old dog's old tricks block the new ones. In retroactive interference ('retro' means backward) later learning disrupts recall of earlier material. Benton Underwood (1957) showed how strong the proactive kind is: people who had already learned many lists in the laboratory forgot a new list far more over a day than people learning their first list, so much of the forgetting came from the old lists, not from time. Example: Arjun has typed on a QWERTY keyboard for twenty years; when he tries a new layout, his fingers keep hitting the old keys. That is proactive interference. If, after months on the new layout, he can no longer type well on QWERTY, that is retroactive interference. Option c is right because the saying describes old habits (earlier learning) blocking the learning of new tricks, which is the forward, old-disrupts-new direction. Exam trap: the two terms are easy to reverse; remember PRO = old disrupts new and RETRO = new disrupts old, then ask which learning is suffering. Here it is the new learning, so the answer is proactive.", "traps": {"a": "Repression is Sigmund Freud's (1915) motivated forgetting, in which threatening memories are pushed out of awareness; an old dog's trouble with new tricks has nothing to do with anxiety-provoking material.", "b": "Retroactive interference runs backward: new learning disrupts recall of old material. The saying describes the opposite direction, old habits blocking new learning.", "d": "'Pass interference' is a foul in American football, not a theory of forgetting; it is a filler that borrows the word interference."}}, {"q": "The overriding Gestalt principle is:", "concept": "The answer is d: the overriding Gestalt principle is simplicity, the law of Pragnanz (good figure). Gestalt psychology held that the whole we perceive is different from the sum of its parts, and that the visual system actively organises what reaches the eye. Max Wertheimer (1923) described the laws by which elements are grouped: proximity (things near each other go together), similarity (things alike in colour, shape or size go together), closure (gaps are filled in to make a complete figure), good continuation (smooth lines are followed) and common fate (things moving together belong together). Kurt Koffka, in Principles of Gestalt Psychology (1935), then treated Pragnanz as the master law: of all the ways a pattern could be organised, we see the simplest, most regular and most stable one the conditions allow. The other laws are particular cases of it. Closure, for instance, works because a whole circle is a simpler figure than a broken arc. Example: Meera sees the Olympic logo as five overlapping rings rather than as a dozen odd curved pieces, because rings are the simplest reading of the pattern. Option d is right because simplicity (good form) is the principle that sits above and explains the other grouping laws, which is why textbooks call it the overriding or fundamental Gestalt law. Exam trap: closure, similarity and proximity are all real Gestalt laws, so each looks correct; the stem asks for the one that governs them all, and that is Pragnanz, also called the law of good figure or the law of simplicity.", "traps": {"a": "Closure is the Gestalt law that we fill in gaps to see a complete figure (a broken circle is seen as a circle); it is one particular case of Pragnanz, not the overriding law.", "b": "Similarity is the law that elements alike in colour, shape or size are grouped together; it is one of Max Wertheimer's grouping laws, which Pragnanz governs.", "c": "Proximity is the law that elements close together in space or time are grouped; it is a specific grouping law, not the master principle."}}, {"q": "Although STM stores information in terms of physical qualities, LTM stores information in terms of:", "concept": "The answer is b: long-term memory (LTM) stores information mainly in terms of semantic codes, that is, by meaning. In the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), information passes from sensory memory to short-term memory (STM), which holds a few items for about 18 to 30 seconds, and, with rehearsal, into LTM, which is large and lasting. The stores also differ in how they code material. STM leans on physical codes, mostly acoustic (how words sound), while LTM leans on meaning. Alan Baddeley (1966) showed this with word lists. Lists of similar-sounding words (man, mad, cap, can) were hard to recall straight away, so STM was confused by sound. Lists of similar-meaning words (big, huge, large, wide) were hard to recall after a delay, so LTM was confused by meaning. Example: a week after a lecture, Riya can tell you the gist of what was said, but not the exact words; she has kept the meaning and lost the sound. LTM can hold sounds and images too (you know a song's tune and your mother's face), but meaning is its dominant code. Option b is right because semantic coding is the typical form of storage in LTM, in contrast to the physical (acoustic or visual) coding of STM that the stem mentions. Exam trap: acoustic codes belong mainly to STM, the very store the stem contrasts LTM with; 'All of the above' fails because LTM is described by its dominant code, and attitudes are not a memory code at all.", "traps": {"a": "Acoustic codes (coding by sound) are the main code of short-term memory, as Reuben Conrad (1964) and Alan Baddeley (1966) showed; the stem sets STM's physical codes against a different code for LTM.", "c": "Attitudes are evaluative dispositions towards objects, a social psychology concept, not a form in which memory encodes information.", "d": "LTM can hold some sounds and images, but its dominant code is meaning; since acoustic codes belong mainly to STM and attitudes are not a code, 'All of the above' cannot be right."}}, {"q": "What are the functions of cues in discrimination learning?", "concept": "The answer is d: in discrimination learning, cues act as signals telling the learner which behaviour will be reinforced or punished. B. F. Skinner (1938) called such a cue a discriminative stimulus (S-D): a stimulus in whose presence a response has been reinforced, so the response becomes more likely whenever it appears. A stimulus signalling that the response will not be reinforced is an S-delta; one signalling that it will be punished is a discriminative stimulus for punishment. When behaviour occurs in the presence of the S-D but not of the S-delta, it is under stimulus control. This is how discrimination is learned: the organism comes to respond differently to different stimuli because they predict different consequences. In the laboratory, a pigeon rewarded for pecking only when a key is lit green soon pecks at green and ignores red. Example: Kabir has learned that asking for sweets works when his grandmother is at home but earns a scolding when his father is there; his grandmother's presence is the S-D and his father's is a discriminative stimulus for punishment. The opposite process is generalisation, responding in the same way to similar stimuli. Option d is right because the whole function of a discriminative cue is to carry information about likely consequences, so that the right behaviour appears in the right setting. Exam trap: do not confuse discrimination with generalisation, and note that the cue does not elicit the response automatically, as a conditioned stimulus does in Ivan Pavlov's (1927) classical conditioning; it only sets the occasion for a response whose consequences have been learned.", "traps": {"a": "Cues work by signalling consequences, not by rewiring the brain; lasting synaptic change (such as long-term potentiation) is thought to underlie learning in general and is not the special function of a discriminative cue.", "b": "Forgetting is explained by decay or interference; a discriminative cue does the opposite, helping the learner produce the right response at the right time.", "c": "Discriminative cues are what make discrimination learning possible; irrelevant distracting stimuli are a different thing and are not the cues the stem asks about."}}, {"q": "Each of the following is a belief of stage theorist except:", "concept": "The answer is b: stage theorists do not hold that the differences between stages are merely quantitative; that is the continuity view. Developmental psychology asks whether change is continuous (gradual and quantitative, like a ramp: more knowledge, faster processing, a bigger vocabulary) or discontinuous (a series of qualitatively different stages, like a staircase). Stage theorists, such as Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) for cognition, Sigmund Freud (1905 to 1923) for psychosexual development, Erik Erikson (1950) for psychosocial development and Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) for moral reasoning, take the discontinuous side. They share three beliefs. First, each stage is qualitatively different: a child at a new stage thinks in a new way, not just more of the old way. Second, every child passes through the same stages in the same invariant order, in every culture. Third, the stages are largely set by biological maturation and unfold on a built-in timetable; experience can speed or slow them, but not reorder them. Example: when Arjun, at about 7, realises that water poured into a tall thin glass is still the same amount, Piaget would say his thinking has changed in kind, not that he simply knows more facts. Learning theorists and information-processing theorists, by contrast, see development as continuous, quantitative growth. Option b is right because 'the differences are quantitative' is the hallmark of the continuity view, so it is the one statement a stage theorist would reject. Exam trap: options a and b differ by one word; qualitative change means a new kind of thinking (the stage view), while quantitative change means more of the same (the continuity view).", "traps": {"a": "Qualitative differences (a new kind of thinking at each stage) are the core belief of stage theorists such as Jean Piaget, so this statement fits them and is not the exception.", "c": "The same stages in the same invariant order is a defining belief of stage theory (Piaget's and Kohlberg's sequences are said to be universal), so it is not the exception.", "d": "Stage theorists see the stages as largely set by biological maturation that unfolds on a timetable, so this statement also fits them."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is characteristic of pre-operational child?", "concept": "The answer is d: the preoperational child is both egocentric and uses transductive reasoning, so (A) and (B) is correct. In Jean Piaget's (1896 to 1980) theory, the preoperational stage runs from about 2 to 7 years. The child can now use symbols (words, images, pretend play) but cannot yet perform operations, mental actions that are logical and reversible. Several limits follow. Egocentrism is the failure to take another person's point of view. In the three mountains task, Jean Piaget and Barbel Inhelder (1956) asked children to pick the picture showing what a doll on the far side of a model could see; four-year-olds usually chose their own view. Transductive reasoning runs from one particular event to another, with no general rule, so two things that happen together are treated as cause and effect. Piaget's own daughter, having missed her nap, insisted that it could not be afternoon. Other features are centration, animism, irreversibility and the lack of conservation. Example: five-year-old Meera thinks her baby brother fell ill because she was cross with him (transductive), and she hides by covering her eyes, sure that if she cannot see you, you cannot see her (egocentric). Option d is right because both A and B are classic preoperational features, while C belongs to a later stage. Exam trap: abstract, hypothetical thought appears only in the formal operational stage, at about 11 years and above; picking A or B alone misses that both are true.", "traps": {"a": "Egocentrism (shown in the three mountains task) is a true preoperational feature, but choosing it alone misses transductive reasoning, which is also true.", "b": "Transductive reasoning (particular to particular) is a true preoperational feature, but choosing it alone misses egocentrism, which is also correct.", "c": "Abstract, hypothetical thought belongs to Piaget's formal operational stage (about 11 years and above), not to the preoperational child."}}, {"q": "A research study in a realistic situation in which one or more independent variables are manipulated by the experimenter under as carefully controlled conditions as the situation permits is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: the stem is the classic definition of a field experiment. Fred Kerlinger, in Foundations of Behavioral Research (first edition 1964), defined it almost word for word: a research study in a realistic situation in which one or more independent variables (IVs) are manipulated by the experimenter under as carefully controlled conditions as the situation permits. Two features matter. First, it is a true experiment: the researcher manipulates the IV and, where possible, assigns people to conditions at random, so cause and effect can be inferred. Second, it happens in the real world (a train, a school, a market), where people often do not know they are being studied. This gives higher external validity and fewer demand characteristics than a laboratory experiment, at the cost of less control over extraneous variables. The classic example is the New York subway study of Irving Piliavin, Judith Rodin and Jane Piliavin (1969): a confederate collapsed in a carriage, appearing either ill (carrying a cane) or drunk, and observers recorded who helped and how fast. Example: Kabir, a school psychologist, has two sections of Class 8 use different revision methods for a month and compares their marks; he manipulates the IV inside a real school. Option a is right because the stem names both defining elements: a realistic setting and a manipulated IV with as much control as the setting allows. Exam trap: a field study also takes place in a natural setting but manipulates nothing; the manipulation is what makes it an experiment.", "traps": {"b": "'Situation experiment' is not a recognised research design; it is a filler built from the stem's word 'situation'.", "c": "A case study is an intensive study of one person, family or unit (such as Sigmund Freud's Little Hans, 1909), with no manipulation of an independent variable.", "d": "An observation study records behaviour, often in natural settings, without manipulating any variable, so it lacks the experimental manipulation the stem describes."}}, {"q": "What BEST distinguishes the infancy stage from other stages of development?", "concept": "The answer is a: what best distinguishes infancy is that physical growth is more rapid in the first year than at any later stage. A newborn's weight roughly doubles by about 5 months and triples by the first birthday, and body length rises by about half in the first year. No later period comes close; even the adolescent growth spurt adds far less in relative terms. Growth in infancy follows two trends. In the cephalocaudal trend (head to tail) the head grows first and fastest, which is why a baby's head is about a quarter of its body length. In the proximodistal trend growth runs from the trunk outward to the arms, hands and fingers. Example: baby Riya weighs 3 kg at birth, about 6 kg at 5 months and about 9 kg on her first birthday; no later year of her life will see her weight triple. Option a is right because rapid physical growth is the defining feature of the first year after birth, while each of the other options makes a false claim. Exam trap: infancy is also a period of rich emotional development (the social smile at about 6 to 8 weeks, stranger anxiety, attachment), so any option saying emotions are absent in infancy, or finished by its end, is wrong. Key note: growth is faster still before birth, in the prenatal period, so the claim holds for the stages after birth; a stands.", "traps": {"b": "There is no '5 times' figure for cognitive growth in infancy; the number is invented, and cognitive change continues through childhood and adolescence (Jean Piaget's later stages).", "c": "Emotions keep developing well after infancy: self-conscious emotions such as pride and guilt, emotion regulation and empathy grow through childhood and adolescence.", "d": "Infancy is full of emotional development, including the social smile at about 6 to 8 weeks, stranger anxiety and attachment (John Bowlby, 1969; Mary Ainsworth, 1978), so this is false."}}, {"q": "Using Jung's theory, 'a wild man' professional wrestler would be seen as an expression of:", "concept": "The answer is c: in the theory of Carl Jung (1875 to 1961), a 'wild man' wrestler would be read as an expression of the wild man archetype. Jung broke with Sigmund Freud in 1913 and divided the unconscious into two layers. The personal unconscious holds one person's forgotten and repressed experiences. Beneath it lies the collective unconscious, inherited and shared by all humans, the deposit of our ancestors' experience. Its contents are archetypes: universal, inborn patterns (a readiness to respond, not inherited memories) that show up in myths, fairy tales, dreams and popular culture across the world. Jung's best-known archetypes are the persona (social mask), the shadow (the dark, animal side), the anima (the feminine side of a man), the animus (the masculine side of a woman) and the self (wholeness), along with the hero, the great mother and the trickster. Later Jungian writers, notably Robert Bly in Iron John (1990), described the Wild Man as an archetype of untamed masculine energy. Example: Kabir cheers a wrestler who storms into the ring wild-haired and roaring; a Jungian would say the act grips crowds everywhere because it taps a universal image. Option c is right because Jung explains such recurring, culture-wide figures as expressions of archetypes in the collective unconscious. Exam trap: the stem says 'using Jung's theory', so Freudian (id) and environmental (upbringing) answers are out, and the anima, though Jungian, is the feminine side of a man. Key note: 'wild man' is not among Jung's best-known named archetypes, and a shadow reading is also possible, but of these options only c names a Jungian archetype; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "The id is Sigmund Freud's inborn, pleasure-seeking part of the personality, not part of Jung's theory; the stem asks for a Jungian explanation.", "b": "The anima is a Jungian archetype, but it is the feminine side of a man's psyche, the opposite of a wild, macho stage persona.", "d": "A harsh upbringing is an environmental explanation drawn from one person's history; Jung explains culture-wide figures by inherited archetypes."}}, {"q": "Cattell believed that pathological personalities are of two kinds; those with one or more abnormal traits and those:", "concept": "The answer is b: Raymond B. Cattell held that pathological personalities either have one or more abnormal traits or have normal traits at an extreme, unbalanced level. Raymond B. Cattell (1905 to 1998) used factor analysis to find source traits, the deep, stable factors beneath behaviour; 16 of them make up his Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949). He then factor-analysed clinical as well as normal samples and concluded that disturbed people differ from normal people in two ways. Some have the normal traits plus one or more abnormal (pathological) traits that normal people lack; textbook accounts count 12 such factors, seven of them kinds of depression, plus others such as paranoia, psychopathic deviation and schizophrenia. Others have no extra trait at all: their pathology lies in normal traits carried to an extreme, or in an imbalance among them. Cattell's Clinical Analysis Questionnaire (CAQ; first manual 1971, later developed with Samuel Krug) measures both sets, the 16 normal factors and the 12 clinical ones. Example: Arjun is extremely high on apprehension and extremely low on emotional stability; each trait is normal in itself, but the extreme combination is disabling. Option b is right because the second kind of pathological personality in Cattell's scheme is the person with an extreme imbalance of normal traits. Exam trap: Cattell's model is dimensional and statistical, so do not import Freudian ideas (defence mechanisms) or a 'born defective' notion into it; and pathological people keep their normal traits rather than lacking them.", "traps": {"a": "'Born defective' echoes old degeneration theories of madness, not Cattell's model, in which abnormal traits are measured dimensions with both genetic and environmental roots.", "c": "Cattell said pathological people have the same normal traits as everyone else, sometimes with extra abnormal ones; no one lacks the normal traits altogether.", "d": "Inadequate defence mechanisms is a psychoanalytic explanation of pathology (Sigmund Freud; Anna Freud, 1936), not part of Cattell's factor-analytic trait theory."}}, {"q": "Sems are:", "concept": "The answer is a: sems are learned or acquired traits. Raymond B. Cattell (1905 to 1998) sorted traits by function into ability traits (how well we pursue goals, such as intelligence), temperament traits (emotional style) and dynamic traits (what moves us to act). Dynamic traits come in two main kinds. Ergs are innate, biological drives that humans share with other animals, such as hunger, sex, fear, curiosity, gregariousness and self-assertion. Sentiments, which Cattell himself shortened to sems (as do textbooks such as Jess Feist and Gregory Feist's Theories of Personality), are learned patterns of attitudes built around an important object or institution, such as a career, religion, the family, the country or the self. They are environmental-mould traits, formed by experience, and one sem can satisfy several ergs at once. The most important is the self-sentiment, which integrates the others. Cattell linked the levels by subsidiation: specific attitudes serve sentiments, and sentiments serve ergs. The whole web of links is the dynamic lattice. Example: Meera wants to revise tonight (an attitude); this serves her career sentiment (a sem), which in turn serves her ergs of self-assertion and security. Option a is right because sems are the acquired, learned side of Cattell's dynamic traits, in contrast to the inborn ergs. Exam trap: examiners swap the two terms; ergs are innate (think biological energy) and sems are learned; and both are dynamic traits, not ability traits.", "traps": {"b": "Innate drives or motives are Cattell's ergs (hunger, sex, fear, curiosity), the inborn counterpart of the learned sems.", "c": "'Fourth-order traits' is not a standard term in Cattell's theory, which builds from surface traits to source (primary) traits and then to broader second-order global factors; sems are classed by function as dynamic traits, not by factor order.", "d": "Ability traits, such as fluid and crystallised intelligence, describe how efficiently a person pursues goals; they form a separate class from the dynamic traits to which sems belong."}}, {"q": "The theory of Cattell rates:", "concept": "The answer is a: Cattell's theory rates high on biological determinants of personality. Personality theory textbooks rate each theory on its concept of humanity (free will versus determinism, causality versus teleology, biological versus social influences and so on) and on its scientific usefulness. Raymond B. Cattell (1905 to 1998) built his theory from factor analysis and behaviour genetics. Using his multiple abstract variance analysis (MAVA), which compares twins and siblings reared together and apart, he estimated how much of each trait is inherited, and he reported substantial heritability for several traits and especially high values for intelligence. He also split source traits by origin into constitutional traits (rooted in biology) and environmental-mould traits (learned). So the theory gives heredity a strong role and rates high, even very high, on biological influences. Its picture of people is deterministic: the specification equation predicts behaviour from a person's traits weighted by the situation, so free will and teleology (future goals pulling behaviour forward) play little or no part. On usefulness, the theory rates high on generating research, because its precise, measurable factors and the 16PF led to a large body of studies. Example: if Kabir and his identical twin, raised in different cities, both score high on boldness, Cattell would read this as evidence of a genetic contribution. Option a is right because heavy reliance on heredity and biology is exactly how the theory is rated. Exam trap: teleology and free will are emphases of humanistic and Adlerian theories, and the research rating for factor theories is high, not low.", "traps": {"b": "Teleology (behaviour pulled by future goals) is stressed by Alfred Adler (fictional finalism, 1912) and the humanists; Cattell explains behaviour by traits and situations, so his theory does not rate high here.", "c": "Factor theories rate high, not low, on generating research: Cattell's measurable factors and the 16PF produced a large body of studies.", "d": "Cattell's model is deterministic, predicting behaviour from traits and situations, so it leaves little room for free will, an emphasis of humanists such as Carl Rogers (1961)."}}, {"q": "Which of these can not be used in a Likert scale?", "concept": "The answer is d: none of these labels is barred, so every listed response can be used in a Likert scale. Rensis Likert (1932) introduced the method of summated ratings. A Likert scale is a set of statements about an attitude object; for each statement the respondent chooses one of a fixed number of ordered options showing how far they agree. The classic set has five points: strongly agree, agree, neither agree nor disagree (undecided), disagree and strongly disagree, scored 5 to 1 (reversed for unfavourable statements). The item scores are then summed into one attitude score. The anchor wording is flexible. 'Agree' is a standard point. 'Neither agree nor disagree' is the usual neutral midpoint; some researchers drop it to force a choice (a 4-point or 6-point scale), but it is allowed and common. The top anchor is often written 'totally agree' or 'completely agree' instead of 'strongly agree', especially in translated questionnaires, and Likert's own 1932 scales used strongly approve, approve, undecided, disapprove and strongly disapprove. Example: Riya builds a scale on attitudes to online classes with the anchors totally disagree, disagree, neither agree nor disagree, agree and totally agree; it is still a Likert scale, because the format is ordered agreement and the scores are summed. Option d is right because agree, totally agree and neither agree nor disagree can all serve as Likert responses, so none of them is excluded. Exam trap: do not pick the neutral midpoint just because some scales leave it out; dropping it is a design choice, not a rule. Key note: some readers argue that 'totally agree' is not a standard Likert label and so pick b, but the item hinges on 'totally agree' being accepted as a variant of 'strongly agree', which it widely is; d stands.", "traps": {"a": "'Agree' is one of the five classic Likert points, between strongly agree and the neutral midpoint, so it can be used.", "b": "'Totally agree' is a common wording for the top anchor (strongly agree), especially in translated scales, so it can be used.", "c": "'Neither agree nor disagree' is the standard neutral midpoint of a 5-point Likert scale; it may be dropped by choice, but it is not forbidden."}}, {"q": "Which of these isn't attitude statement?", "concept": "The answer is b: 'Some people like to bully others' is not an attitude statement. An attitude is an evaluative disposition (a liking or disliking, with its beliefs and action tendencies) towards an object, person, group or idea. An attitude statement must therefore let respondents reveal their own evaluation: whether they agree should depend on how they feel. Allen Edwards (1957), in Techniques of Attitude Scale Construction, gathered rules for writing such statements. Among them: avoid statements of fact, or statements that could be read as factual; avoid statements that almost everyone or almost no one would endorse; refer to the present rather than the past; keep statements short, clear and simple, with one idea each. 'Some people like to bully others' breaks the first rule. It is a factual claim about other people, true as a matter of observation, so a kind person and a bully would both agree with it, and the answer tells you nothing about the respondent's own attitude to bullying. Compare 'Bullying is sometimes justified', which does reveal an attitude. Example: Kabir, writing a scale on attitudes to bullying for Class 9 students, drops this item at the tryout stage because everyone ticks 'agree'. Option b is right because it states a fact about others rather than the respondent's own feeling, belief or intention. Exam trap: first-person wording is a clue, but the real test is evaluation; 'I love sweets' and 'I wish I could be more helpful' express how the speaker feels, while a general observation about the world does not.", "traps": {"a": "'Somehow, I don't regard myself as an achiever' is a self-evaluation, the respondent's attitude towards himself or herself, so it is an attitude statement.", "c": "'I love sweets' directly expresses a liking (the affective component of attitude) towards an object, so it is an attitude statement.", "d": "'I wish I could be more helpful' expresses an evaluation and a behavioural intention about helping, so it qualifies as an attitude statement."}}, {"q": "Research participants who are shown pictures of extremely attractive individuals and then are asked to rate another person, often show a(n):", "concept": "The answer is b: people who first see very attractive individuals and then rate someone else show a contrast effect. A contrast effect is a judgement bias in which a stimulus is judged against what came just before it: after something extreme, an ordinary stimulus seems further from it than it really is. It runs through perception (lukewarm water feels cold after hot water) and social judgement alike. Douglas Kenrick and Sara Gutierres (1980) tested it on attractiveness. Male students who were watching Charlie's Angels, a television show starring three glamorous actresses, rated a photo of an average-looking woman, offered as a possible blind date, as less attractive than men who had not been watching. Douglas Kenrick, Sara Gutierres and Laurie Goldberg (1989) reported that men who looked at centrefold photographs then rated their own partners as less attractive and said they loved them less, though later, much larger replications did not find this partner effect. Example: Arjun scrolls through photos of film stars for half an hour and then finds his friend's wedding photos oddly ordinary; the faces have not changed, his comparison standard has. Option b is right because the lowered rating comes from comparing the new person with the extremely attractive images just seen. Exam trap: do not confuse the contrast effect with the halo effect, in which one good trait (such as good looks) makes us rate a person well on unrelated traits; a halo spreads a judgement, whereas contrast pushes it away from a reference point.", "traps": {"a": "'Rating discrepancy' is not a named bias; it merely describes a difference between ratings without explaining it, so it is a filler.", "c": "'Latency effect' is not a social judgement bias (latency means delay, as in reaction time); it is a filler with a scientific sound.", "d": "'Associated effect' is not a recognised term in social psychology; it is a filler, and the lowered ratings are explained by contrast."}}, {"q": "If there was no CO₂ in the atmosphere, the earth's atmosphere would be:", "concept": "The answer is a: without carbon dioxide (CO2) the earth's temperature would be lower than at present. The ground, warmed by sunlight, gives off heat as infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases (water vapour, CO2, methane) absorb much of this outgoing heat and send part of it back down. John Tyndall (1859) showed in the laboratory that CO2 absorbs heat, and Svante Arrhenius (1896) calculated how changes in CO2 would change the earth's temperature. Without any greenhouse gases, the average surface temperature would be about minus 18 degrees Celsius instead of about plus 15. CO2 matters more than its small share suggests: warmer air holds more water vapour, so removing the CO2 would let much of the water vapour condense out, and the planet would cool sharply. Option a is right because less CO2 means a weaker greenhouse effect and a colder earth. Exam trap: CO2's warming does not depend on oxygen. Key note: the stem is garbled and says 'atmosphere' where it means temperature; read as the earth's temperature, a stands.", "traps": {"b": "CO2 is a major greenhouse gas, so removing it would cut the trapping of outgoing heat; the temperature could not stay the same.", "c": "A higher temperature follows more CO2, not less; removing CO2 would weaken the greenhouse effect and cool the earth.", "d": "CO2 traps heat because its own molecules absorb infrared radiation; the oxygen content of the air does not control this effect."}}, {"q": "What are the main components of Spearman's two factor theory?", "concept": "The answer is a: Spearman's two-factor theory has two components, general intelligence (g) and specific intelligence (s). Charles Spearman (1904), in his paper 'General intelligence, objectively determined and measured', noticed that schoolchildren's scores on very different measures (classics, French, mathematics, pitch discrimination) all correlated positively, a pattern now called the positive manifold. Using an early form of factor analysis, he explained it by a general factor, g, shared by all mental tasks and pictured as a kind of mental energy, plus a specific factor, s, unique to each test. Every test score therefore reflects g plus that test's own s. Tests that draw heavily on g, such as reasoning, correlate strongly with one another; tests that rely more on s, such as simple sensory tasks, correlate less. Spearman developed the theory in The Abilities of Man (1927). Example: Riya does well in mathematics, English and science alike, which Spearman would credit to a high g, while her outstanding skill at drawing reflects a specific factor. Option a is right because the two factors in the theory's name are exactly general intelligence and specific intelligence. Exam trap: primary mental abilities belong to Louis Leon Thurstone (1938), who found about seven group factors and challenged a single g; broad group factors such as verbal-educational and spatial-mechanical ability belong to Philip Vernon's (1950) hierarchical model.", "traps": {"b": "Visual and spatial abilities are group factors that sit below g in hierarchical models (Philip Vernon's spatial-mechanical factor), not the two components of Spearman's theory.", "c": "Primary abilities are Louis Leon Thurstone's (1938) seven primary mental abilities, a multiple-factor rival to Spearman; 'secondary abilities' is not part of either theory.", "d": "Emotional abilities belong to emotional intelligence (Peter Salovey and John Mayer, 1990), which Spearman's purely cognitive theory did not include."}}, {"q": "How many SD above the mean IQ score did Terman and Oden start to assess individuals as being gifted?", "concept": "The answer is d: giftedness is conventionally set at 2 standard deviations (SD) above the mean IQ, an IQ of about 130. Lewis Terman (1877 to 1956), who produced the Stanford-Binet test (1916), began his Genetic Studies of Genius in 1921. He identified 1,528 California children with very high Stanford-Binet IQs, mostly 135 to 140 and above (mean about 151), and followed them for the rest of their lives; they became known as the 'Termites'. Melita Oden co-wrote the follow-ups, The Gifted Child Grows Up (1947) and The Gifted Group at Mid-Life (1959). The study overturned the myth that bright children are frail and odd: as adults they were, on average, healthier, better adjusted and more successful than others. On modern tests with a mean of 100 and an SD of 15, an IQ of 130 lies 2 SD above the mean and is reached by only about 2 per cent of people; IQ 70, 2 SD below the mean, marks the range of intellectual disability. Example: Meera scores 132 on a Wechsler scale; at more than 2 SD above the mean, she would usually qualify as gifted. Option d is right because 2 SD above the mean is the textbook gifted cut-off linked to Terman and Oden's group. Exam trap: 1 SD above (IQ 115) is merely above average, and 3 SD above (IQ 145) is far rarer, about 0.1 per cent of people. Key note: Terman's own cut-off was about IQ 135 to 140, between 2 and 2.5 SD, so '2 SD' is the textbook rounding and the closest option; d stands.", "traps": {"a": "1 SD above the mean (IQ 115) marks only the upper end of the average range; about 16 per cent of people score this high, far too many to count as gifted.", "b": "'No SD above the mean' is simply the mean, IQ 100, the score of the average person, not a giftedness cut-off.", "c": "3 SD above the mean (IQ 145) is used by some for 'highly gifted', but Terman and Oden's gifted group is described in textbooks at about 2 SD above the mean."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is most consistent with the two factor theory of emotion?", "concept": "The answer is a: the two-factor theory holds that the same physiological arousal can be interpreted in different ways, producing different emotions. Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962) proposed that an emotion has two ingredients: physiological arousal, which sets how strong the feeling is, and a cognitive label taken from the situation, which decides which emotion it is. Because arousal (racing heart, sweating, trembling) is much the same in fear, anger and joy, the label does the work of telling them apart. In their classic experiment, students were injected with adrenaline (epinephrine), described as a vitamin; some were told its real effects, others were told nothing or misinformed, and a placebo group got saline. Each then waited with a stooge who acted either euphoric or angry. The aroused but uninformed students, who had no explanation for their racing hearts, caught the stooge's mood most, while the informed students, who could blame the injection, did so least. Example: Arjun's heart is pounding after he runs up four flights of stairs; if he meets a friend who is laughing, he may feel elated, but if someone shouts at him, the same arousal may feel like anger. Option a is right because it states the core claim: one state of arousal, different interpretations, different emotions. Exam trap: match each sequence to its theory; arousal and emotion at the same time is Cannon-Bard, arousal first and then emotion is James-Lange, and emotion first and then arousal is the common-sense view.", "traps": {"b": "Arousal and emotion occurring at the same time, triggered independently by the thalamus, is the Cannon-Bard theory (Walter Cannon, 1927; Philip Bard, 1928).", "c": "Arousal first, with the emotion being our perception of the bodily change, is the James-Lange theory (William James, 1884; Carl Lange, 1885); it has no cognitive label drawn from the situation.", "d": "Emotion first and bodily arousal afterwards is the common-sense view that William James (1884) rejected; the two-factor theory makes arousal plus its interpretation the source of emotion."}}, {"q": "Thorndike's Multifactor theory of intelligence is at one extreme of the interpretations regarding the nature of:", "concept": "The answer is c: Edward Thorndike's (1927) multifactor theory sits at one extreme of the interpretations regarding the nature of mental organisation, that is, how the abilities that make up intelligence are structured. Theorists who correlated many tests asked whether the abilities behind them form one general power, a few broad group factors or a host of separate bits. Thorndike gave the most splintered answer. For him intelligence is made up of a multitude of separate, minute elements of ability, each a set of stimulus-response connections. Any mental act uses a number of these elements together, two tasks correlate only to the extent that they share elements, and there is no general intelligence: a brighter person simply has more connections. The theory is therefore called atomistic. Even so, Thorndike grouped tasks into abstract, mechanical and social intelligence. At the other extreme stands Charles Spearman's (1904) two-factor theory, in which all intellectual activity depends mainly on one general factor, g, plus a specific factor, s, for each task; group-factor theories such as that of Louis Leon Thurstone (1938) sit in between. Example: Kabir repairs engines brilliantly but is average at abstract puzzles; Thorndike would call these largely separate abilities. Option c is right because the range from multifactor to two-factor theories is a range of views on how the mind's abilities are organised, which is exactly the phrase the textbook uses. Exam trap: the opposite pole is Spearman's g, not Thurstone's primary mental abilities, which are a middle position; and do not mix up Thorndike (multifactor) with Thurstone (primary mental abilities). Key note: the stem copies a sentence from Frank S. Freeman's Theory and Practice of Psychological Testing (third edition, 1962), which reads 'the nature of mental organization', so c is the answer the paper intends. Option b, intellectual organisation, means much the same thing here; if you picked it you understood the idea, but on this paper c is the safe mark, and the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Motor organisation concerns the control and coordination of movement (motor development and the motor system), not the structure of intelligence.", "b": "'Intellectual organisation' means much the same as mental organisation here, but the sentence this stem is copied from (Frank S. Freeman, 1962) says 'mental organization', so c is the intended answer (see the Key note).", "d": "Reasoning is one specific ability inside the structure (one of Louis Leon Thurstone's primary mental abilities), not the broad question of how intelligence as a whole is organised."}}, {"q": "In interpersonal communication ethics:", "concept": "The answer is c: in interpersonal communication, ethics are important. Communication ethics are the moral standards for how we speak to, listen to and treat one another: being honest, respecting the other person's dignity and right to choose, keeping confidences, giving accurate information and not manipulating, deceiving or bullying. The National Communication Association's Credo for Ethical Communication (1999) puts truthfulness, accuracy, honesty and reason at the centre of good communication, and asks us to understand and respect other communicators before judging their messages. Ethical communication builds trust, and trust lowers the barriers (suspicion, defensiveness, hidden agendas) that block understanding. Example: Meera tells her friend Kabir honestly that she cannot lend him money, instead of inventing an excuse; the honesty keeps the friendship open and trusting. Option c is right because ethics decide whether communication is truthful, respectful and trusted. Exam trap: the three wrong options all reverse the point by calling ethics irrelevant or a hindrance; in fact ethics support honesty and understanding rather than standing in their way.", "traps": {"a": "Ethics are always a consideration, because every message can help or harm the listener; calling them irrelevant ignores honesty, respect and consent, the core of interpersonal communication ethics.", "b": "Barriers to understanding are things like noise, jargon, defensiveness and distrust; ethical communication lowers them by building trust, it does not raise them.", "d": "Honesty (truthfulness) is itself one of the central principles of communication ethics, so ethics promote honesty rather than block it."}}, {"q": "Legitimate power is based on the subordinate's perception that the leader has a right to exercise influence because of the leader's:", "concept": "The answer is a: legitimate power rests on the leader's role or position within the organisation. John French and Bertram Raven (1959) described five bases of social power, the sources from which one person's capacity to influence another comes. Legitimate power comes from an accepted right to give orders that goes with a role: subordinates comply because they believe a manager, officer or teacher is entitled to direct them, whoever holds the post. Reward power rests on the ability to give rewards (bonuses, praise), coercive power on the ability to punish, expert power on superior knowledge or skill, and referent power on admiration and identification with the agent. Raven (1965) later added informational power, the persuasive content of the message itself. Legitimate power goes when the role goes: a retired principal cannot order the staff about. In Stanley Milgram's (1963) obedience studies, the experimenter's authority as a scientist in a laboratory worked largely through legitimate power. Example: Arjun submits his report by Friday because his team leader, Priya, asked him to and setting deadlines is part of her job, not because he likes her or fears a penalty. Option a is right because the stem's phrase 'a right to exercise influence' is the definition of legitimate power, and that right comes from the position. Exam trap: a manager usually holds several bases at once, but the 'right' to influence is legitimate power; the ability to reward or punish (option d) is a separate pair of bases, even though it often comes with the position.", "traps": {"b": "Influence based on personal characteristics, charm and being liked or admired is referent power in French and Raven's scheme, not legitimate power.", "c": "Influence based on expertise and knowledge is expert power (the doctor whose advice you follow because she knows more), not a right that comes with a role.", "d": "The ability to reward is reward power and the ability to punish is coercive power; they are two separate bases, resting on control of outcomes rather than on a recognised right to command."}}, {"q": "Behaviours that are persistently aggressive or involve acting out and non compliant behaviours are _____ behaviours.", "concept": "The answer is a: persistently aggressive, acting-out and non-compliant behaviours are called externalising behaviours. Thomas Achenbach (1966), factor-analysing the recorded symptoms of child psychiatric patients, found that children's problems fall into two broad groups. Externalising problems are undercontrolled and directed outward, at other people and the environment: aggression, defiance, temper outbursts, rule-breaking, lying, stealing and hyperactivity. They form the core of oppositional defiant disorder, conduct disorder and, in part, ADHD. Internalising problems are overcontrolled and directed inward, at the self: anxiety, depression, withdrawal, shyness and bodily complaints. Both broad-band dimensions are scored on Achenbach's Child Behavior Checklist, completed by parents, with parallel forms for teachers (the Teacher's Report Form) and for adolescents themselves (the Youth Self-Report). Externalising problems trouble the people around the child, so they are referred more often; internalising problems mainly trouble the child and are easily missed. Example: Rohan, aged 9, hits classmates, refuses the teacher's instructions and throws things when told off, an externalising pattern; his sister Anika, quietly anxious and tearful, shows an internalising one. Option a is right because the stem describes the outward-directed, acting-out cluster, which is the definition of externalising behaviour. Exam trap: 'defiant' and 'antisocial' sound right because the stem mentions non-compliance and aggression, but they are narrower labels (oppositional defiant disorder; conduct disorder and antisocial personality disorder) that sit inside the broader externalising dimension.", "traps": {"b": "Internalising behaviours are the overcontrolled, inward-directed problems (anxiety, depression, withdrawal, somatic complaints), the opposite pole of Achenbach's dimension from aggression and acting out.", "c": "'Defiant' points to oppositional defiant disorder (angry, argumentative, defiant behaviour towards authority), one narrower pattern inside the externalising group; it does not cover aggression and acting out as a whole.", "d": "'Antisocial' names conduct disorder in children or antisocial personality disorder in adults (from age 18, with conduct disorder before 15), a narrower and more serious pattern within the externalising spectrum, not the umbrella term."}}, {"q": "A common feature of panic attacks is hyperventilation and it is due to:", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements describe the hyperventilation that so often comes with panic attacks. Hyperventilation means over-breathing, breathing faster or deeper than the body needs: a dysfunctional breathing pattern (option a). Breathing out too much carbon dioxide lowers its level in the blood (hypocapnia). Carbon dioxide forms carbonic acid, so losing it makes the blood less acidic: blood pH rises, a state called respiratory alkalosis (option b). Through the Bohr effect, haemoglobin then holds on to oxygen more tightly, and blood vessels in the brain narrow, so oxygen is delivered less effectively to the body's cells (option c). The result is dizziness, light-headedness, tingling in the fingers and lips, breathlessness, a racing heart and chest tightness. In David Clark's (1986) cognitive model of panic, the person catastrophically misinterprets these harmless sensations as a heart attack, suffocation or going mad, which raises anxiety and over-breathing further in a vicious circle. Treatment therefore includes breathing retraining and interoceptive exposure, in which the client over-breathes on purpose to learn that the sensations are safe. Example: Neha, in a crowded metro, starts gulping air, feels her fingers tingle and her head spin, and is sure she is dying. Option d is right because the dysfunctional breathing pattern, the rise in pH and the poorer oxygen delivery are all parts of the same hyperventilation picture. Exam trap: many students pick a alone, but b and c are also textbook statements about hyperventilation, so 'All of the above' is the complete answer. Key note: strictly, raised blood pH and poorer oxygen delivery are consequences of hyperventilation rather than its causes, so the stem's 'due to' is loose; the item bundles the cause and the effects of the same breathing problem, and the answer used here, d, stands.", "traps": {"a": "A dysfunctional (over-)breathing pattern is what hyperventilation is, so a is true, but picking it alone leaves out the rise in blood pH (b) and the poorer oxygen delivery (c) that also belong to the picture.", "b": "Rising blood pH (respiratory alkalosis, from blowing off carbon dioxide) is true of hyperventilation, but it is only one part; a and c are also correct, so b alone is incomplete.", "c": "Less effective oxygen delivery to the cells (haemoglobin grips oxygen more tightly when carbon dioxide falls, and brain vessels narrow) is true, but a and b are also correct, so c alone is incomplete."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a predominant evolutionary theory of phobias?", "concept": "The answer is c: biological preparedness is the leading evolutionary theory of phobias. Martin Seligman (1971) argued that evolution has prepared humans to learn fears of things that threatened our ancestors, such as snakes, spiders, heights, deep water and angry faces, far more easily than fears of modern dangers such as electric sockets or cars. Prepared fears can form after a single bad experience, or even from watching someone else, and they resist extinction. This explains why phobias cluster around a short list of ancestral objects. Strong evidence comes from rhesus monkeys: Michael Cook and Susan Mineka (1989) showed laboratory-reared monkeys videos of other monkeys reacting with fear; they learned to fear toy snakes and toy crocodiles, but not flowers or toy rabbits edited into the same footage. Arne Ohman and Susan Mineka (2001) later described an evolved \"fear module\" in the brain that such cues trigger selectively. Example: Riya has never been bitten, yet one scream from her cousin at a garden snake leaves her with a lasting snake phobia, while a far more dangerous shock from a faulty switch leaves no phobia at all. Option c is right because preparedness is the evolutionary explanation that dominates textbook accounts of phobias. Exam trap: a learned fear response (option b) sounds scientific but is plain conditioning with no evolutionary claim, while the non-associative account (option a) is also evolutionary, so students who spot only that word may pick it. Key note: Ross Menzies and John Clarke (1995) proposed non-associative fear acquisition (reviewed by Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies, 2002), in which some fears (of heights, water, strangers) are innate and appear without any learning; it is evolutionary too, but a minority view, and preparedness remains the predominant evolutionary account, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Non-associative fear acquisition is the view, proposed by Ross Menzies and John Clarke (1995) and reviewed by Richie Poulton and Ross Menzies (2002), that some fears are innate and need no learning at all. It is evolutionary, but a minority position, so it is not the predominant theory the stem asks for.", "b": "A learned fear response is classical conditioning, as when John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) conditioned Little Albert to fear a white rat. It is a learning explanation and makes no claim about evolution.", "d": "Specific phobia acquisition is not the name of any theory; specific phobia is a DSM-5 diagnosis. Stanley Rachman's (1977) three pathways (conditioning, vicarious learning, information) explain how phobias are acquired, but they are not an evolutionary theory either."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is most consistent with the biopsychosocial model?", "concept": "The answer is c: the statement that a genetic predisposition leads to mental illness only when environmental and cognitive or emotional factors are also present is the one most consistent with the biopsychosocial model. The psychiatrist George Engel (1977), in a paper in Science, argued that the purely biomedical model cannot explain why people with the same biochemical abnormality differ so much in whether, and how badly, they fall ill. He proposed that every illness arises from interacting biological factors (genes, brain chemistry), psychological factors (thoughts, emotions, coping, learning) and social factors (family, poverty, culture, life stress), so treatment must address all three levels. Option c is the diathesis-stress form of this idea: Paul Meehl (1962), and later Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977), held that an inherited vulnerability (the diathesis) lies dormant until stress triggers it, and neither alone is enough. Example: Kavya has a family history of depression (biological), tends to blame herself for every setback (psychological) and loses her job and her support network in the same year (social); the model says her depression comes from all of these together. Option c is right because it is the only option that combines biological, psychological and social causes and says none alone is sufficient. Exam trap: options a and b both sound scientific, but any statement that blames one level only (genes and brain alone, or society alone) is a single-factor model; the biopsychosocial answer is always the one that combines the levels.", "traps": {"a": "Brain abnormalities, neurotransmitters and genes alone make up the biological (biomedical or medical) model, the very one-level view Engel set out to widen.", "b": "Oppression and poverty as the driving causes is the sociocultural model; it names the social level but leaves out the biological and psychological ones.", "d": "Denying that mental illness exists and calling it an understandable reaction to an abnormal situation is the anti-psychiatry view of Thomas Szasz (The Myth of Mental Illness, 1961) and Ronald D. Laing (The Divided Self, 1960), not the biopsychosocial model, which treats illness as real."}}, {"q": "In an experiment an effect size is:", "concept": "The answer is b: an effect size is an objective and standardised measure of the magnitude of the effect observed in a study. A significance test tells you whether an effect is probably not zero, but the p value depends heavily on sample size: with thousands of participants even a trivial difference becomes 'significant'. An effect size answers the other question, how much, in a unit that does not depend on N and can be compared across studies. The best known is Cohen's d, the difference between two group means divided by their pooled standard deviation; Jacob Cohen (1988) suggested 0.2 as small, 0.5 as medium and 0.8 as large. Other indices are the correlation r (.10, .30 and .50 as small, medium and large) and eta squared, the share of variance an independent variable explains in ANOVA. Effect sizes are also the raw material of meta-analysis, the method Gene Glass (1976) named for combining the results of many studies. Example: Riya's mindfulness group scores 5 points lower on anxiety than her control group, and the pooled SD is 10, so d = 0.5, a medium effect, whatever her p value. Option b is right because it gives the textbook definition: standardised, objective and about the size of the observed effect. Exam trap: effect size is not the same as statistical significance or power, and it says nothing about whether the experiment was run well; options a, c and d all describe the success or quality of the study instead of the size of the effect.", "traps": {"a": "Whether the manipulation worked is checked with a manipulation check (did the stressed group actually feel stressed?), not with an effect size, which measures how big the difference in the dependent variable is.", "c": "Hypothesis (significance) testing decides whether an effect is probably real; the effect size is reported alongside it to show how large the effect is, so the two are different things.", "d": "How well the experiment was carried out is a matter of experimental control and internal validity, not of effect size; a well-run study can still find a tiny effect."}}, {"q": "A blind experiment is one in which:", "concept": "The answer is d: in a blind experiment, the subjects do not know whether they are in the experimental or the control group. Blinding (masking) keeps knowledge of the condition from people whose expectations could bias the results. In a single-blind study only the participants are kept unaware, usually by giving the control group a placebo that looks just like the real treatment. This controls the placebo effect (improving because you believe you are being treated) and demand characteristics, the cues that tell participants what the researcher hopes to find (Martin Orne, 1962). In a double-blind study neither the participants nor the people who give the treatment and record the outcomes know who is in which group, which also controls the experimenter expectancy effects shown by Robert Rosenthal (1966). A triple-blind study also blinds those who analyse the data. Example: Riya tests whether a herbal tea improves sleep; half her participants get the tea and half an identical-tasting placebo tea, and none of them knows which they received, so their sleep reports cannot be swayed by belief alone. Option d is right because keeping participants unaware of their group is the defining feature of a blind (single-blind) experiment. Exam trap: option c describes a double-blind experiment, the stronger design that also blinds the experimenters; it is a blind design too, but 'blind experiment' on its own refers to the basic case in which the participants are unaware. Key note: a double-blind study (option c) is also a kind of blind experiment, so c is arguable; but the textbook definition of a 'blind' experiment, with no 'double', is the single-blind case in which only the participants are kept unaware, so the answer used here, d, stands.", "traps": {"a": "Here the participants know their condition, so placebo effects and their expectations go uncontrolled; that is not what a blind experiment means, since blinding is defined by the participants being unaware.", "b": "When both participants and administrators know who is receiving the treatment, the study is open (unblinded), the opposite of a blind experiment.", "c": "Neither participants nor administrators knowing is the double-blind design, which adds control of experimenter bias; the stem asks for the plain blind (single-blind) experiment, defined by the participants being unaware."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is true?", "concept": "The answer is a: an interaction can occur even when neither independent variable has a main effect. In a factorial design, set out by Ronald Fisher (1935) and analysed with factorial ANOVA, two or more independent variables (IVs) are crossed, so every level of one is combined with every level of the other. A main effect is the effect of one IV averaged over the levels of the other. An interaction exists when the effect of one IV depends on the level of the other; on a graph the lines are not parallel. Main effects and interactions are tested separately and are independent of one another, so any combination can occur. Example: Kabir compares two teaching methods with visual and verbal learners. Method A gives visual learners an average of 80 and verbal learners 60; Method B gives visual learners 60 and verbal learners 80. Each method averages 70 and each type of learner averages 70, so neither IV has a main effect, yet the lines cross completely: the better method depends on the learner. This is a crossover (disordinal) interaction. Option a is right because main effects are averages, and opposite effects at different levels can cancel out in the average while the interaction stays large. Exam trap: students assume an interaction needs at least one main effect, or that two main effects must produce an interaction; neither is true, because the two main effects and the interaction in a two-way design are separate effects.", "traps": {"b": "Two main effects can simply add up, giving parallel lines and no interaction (for example, each IV adds 10 points at every level of the other), so two main effects do not guarantee an interaction.", "c": "This is the reverse of the truth in option a: a crossover interaction can occur with no main effect of either IV, so an individual effect of one IV is not required.", "d": "Option a is a correct statement about factorial designs, so 'None of the answers is correct' cannot be right."}}, {"q": "Measures of central tendency are:", "concept": "The answer is b: measures of central tendency are descriptive statistics that identify the best single value for representing a set of data. Descriptive statistics summarise the data you actually have; inferential statistics (the t test, the F test, chi-square) use a sample to draw conclusions about a population. A measure of central tendency is one score that stands for the whole distribution, its typical or central value. There are three main ones. The mean is the arithmetic average (the sum of the scores divided by N); it uses every score but is pulled by extreme scores. The median is the middle score when the scores are put in order, the 50th percentile; it resists outliers, so it suits skewed data such as income, and ordinal data. The mode is the most frequent score and the only average for nominal data. Example: Meera's quiz marks are 4, 7, 7, 9 and 13: the mean is 8, the median 7 and the mode 7. Option b is right because these measures describe a data set with a single representative value and make no inference beyond it. Exam trap: two separate distinctions are tested at once, descriptive versus inferential and central tendency versus variability; spread (range, variance, standard deviation) is variability, not central tendency, and both kinds of measure are descriptive.", "traps": {"a": "The 'best single value' part is right, but measures of central tendency are descriptive, not inferential; inferential statistics such as t and F generalise from a sample to a population.", "c": "This is wrong twice: the spread of scores is described by measures of variability, and those are descriptive, not inferential.", "d": "Descriptive statistics that identify the spread of scores are measures of variability (range, variance, standard deviation), the partner of central tendency, not central tendency itself."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a characteristic of the mean?", "concept": "The answer is d: 'it is best used with ordinal data' is not a characteristic of the mean. The arithmetic mean (M = ΣX ÷ N) is the sum of the scores divided by their number. It has three classic properties. First, it is the balance point of the distribution: deviations above and below it cancel, so the sum of deviations about the mean is always zero. Second, the sum of squared deviations about the mean is smaller than about any other value, the least-squares property that makes the mean the basis of variance, SD and regression. Third, because it uses every score, it is affected by extreme scores: the marks 4, 7, 7, 9 and 13 have a mean of 8, but adding one score of 40 lifts it to about 13.3. The mean assumes equal distances between scores, so it needs interval or ratio data. Ordinal data, such as class ranks or finishing positions in a race, show only order; the gap between first and second need not equal the gap between second and third, so the median is the proper average, and for nominal data the mode. Example: if Arjun's teacher records only class ranks, the median rank is a fair summary, but a mean rank assumes that the gap between 1st and 2nd equals the gap between 9th and 10th, which ranks cannot tell us. Option d is right because ordinal data suit the median, not the mean. Exam trap: the item asks for what is NOT true; options a, b and c are all genuine properties, so a hurried reader picks the first true statement.", "traps": {"a": "Being pulled by extreme scores is a true characteristic of the mean, and its main weakness; that is why the median is preferred for skewed data.", "b": "Minimising the sum of squared deviations (the least-squares property) is a true characteristic of the mean.", "c": "The sum of deviations about the mean is always zero; this is a true, defining property of the mean as the balance point of the distribution."}}, {"q": "The sum of squared deviations is a good measure of variability except:", "concept": "The answer is c: the sum of squared deviations is a poor measure of variability on its own because it does not take N, the number of scores, into account. The sum of squares (SS) is found by taking each score's deviation from the mean, squaring it and adding up the squares: SS = the sum of (X minus the mean) squared. Squaring removes negative signs and gives extra weight to large deviations. SS is the building block of the variance, the standard deviation, ANOVA and regression. Its flaw is that it keeps growing as scores are added, even when the spread stays the same, so SS values from groups of different sizes cannot be compared. Dividing SS by N (or by N minus 1 for a sample estimate of the population) gives the variance, an average squared deviation, and the square root of the variance gives the standard deviation, back in the original units. Example: Arjun's group of 5 students and Sana's group of 50 have marks spread equally widely around their means, yet Sana's SS is roughly ten times larger simply because she has ten times as many scores. Option c is right because SS ignores the number of scores and so cannot by itself compare variability across groups. Exam trap: option a sounds like a criticism, but measuring deviations from the mean is exactly what makes SS work (the sum of squared deviations about the mean is the smallest possible), so it is a strength, not a flaw.", "traps": {"a": "Using the mean is not a weakness: deviations are measured from the mean by definition, and squared deviations about the mean are at a minimum (the least squares property).", "b": "SS needs only equal intervals, so it is used with interval as well as ratio data (IQ and most test scores are interval); it is not restricted to ratio scales.", "d": "SS is the basis of many other analyses: the variance, the standard deviation, ANOVA (which partitions the total SS) and regression all use it."}}, {"q": "Which of the following descriptive statistical procedures cannot be used with a continuous variable?", "concept": "The answer is a: a simple (ungrouped) frequency distribution is the procedure that cannot sensibly be used with a continuous variable. A frequency distribution lists every score and how many people obtained it. That works for a discrete variable, which takes only separate values (number of children, number of errors, a 1 to 5 rating), because the scores repeat and the table stays short. A continuous variable, such as reaction time, height or weight, can take any value in a range, limited only by the precision of measurement, so almost every score is different (0.512, 0.518, 0.523 seconds and so on). A table of every value with a frequency of 1 beside each would be as long as the raw data and show no pattern. Continuous data are therefore summarised in a grouped frequency distribution, with scores pooled into equal class intervals, or drawn as a histogram or frequency polygon. The median and the mean work well with continuous data, which are usually interval or ratio. Example: Ishaan records the reaction times of 60 students to the nearest millisecond and finds 58 different values, so he groups them into ten intervals to see the shape. Option a is right because a simple frequency distribution suits discrete scores, while continuous scores need grouping. Exam trap: option b, the grouped frequency distribution, is exactly the tool designed for continuous data, so do not let the shared words 'frequency distribution' pull you towards it. Key note: once continuous scores are rounded you can technically tally them in a simple frequency table, but the textbook point is that a simple frequency distribution suits discrete data and continuous data need grouping, so the answer used here, a, stands.", "traps": {"b": "A grouped frequency distribution, which pools scores into equal class intervals, is the standard way to summarise continuous data, so it can certainly be used.", "c": "The median, the middle score of the ordered data, can be found for any continuous variable, and it is preferred when the data are skewed.", "d": "The mean is the usual average for continuous interval or ratio data such as reaction time or height, so it can be used."}}, {"q": "Purposive samples contain participants who:", "concept": "The answer is b: purposive samples contain participants who meet specific purposes or criteria related to the aims of the study. Purposive sampling, also called judgemental or purposeful sampling, is a non-probability method: there is no random selection, so the chance of any person being chosen is unknown. Instead the researcher uses knowledge of the topic to hand-pick people who fit criteria set by the research question, such as typical cases, experts, key informants or information-rich cases. It is common in qualitative research, where the aim is depth of understanding rather than generalisation to a population; Michael Quinn Patton (1990) set out many purposeful strategies, such as typical case, extreme case, maximum variation and criterion sampling. Its weakness is bias, since the sample depends on the researcher's judgement, and results cannot be generalised statistically. Example: to study the stress of caring for a child with autism, Dr Lakshmi recruits only mothers whose children have a confirmed autism diagnosis and who have been the main carer for at least two years. Option b is right because the defining feature of purposive sampling is that people are chosen deliberately against criteria tied to the study's purpose. Exam trap: availability alone (option a) is convenience sampling, and representing each group in fixed numbers (option d) is quota sampling; both are also non-probability methods, but neither selects people for fitting the study's aims.", "traps": {"a": "Choosing people simply because they are available to the researcher is convenience (accidental or availability) sampling, which needs no criteria tied to the study's aims.", "c": "Taking in people who happen to be in the field when something unexpected happens is opportunistic (emergent) sampling, in which a qualitative researcher follows new leads during fieldwork (Michael Quinn Patton lists it as one of his purposeful strategies); it describes how particular cases turn up, not the general definition of purposive sampling, which is selection against criteria tied to the study's aims.", "d": "Making sure each group is represented is quota sampling (non-random filling of set numbers for each group), or stratified random sampling if selection within the groups is random."}}, {"q": "If we want to quantify the degree of correlation between academic achievement and creativity, which correlation coefficient shall we use?", "concept": "The answer is d: the choice of coefficient depends on how academic achievement and creativity are measured. Correlation coefficients are matched to the level of measurement and the shape of the data. Karl Pearson's (1896) product-moment correlation, r, suits two variables measured on interval or ratio scales whose relationship is linear and whose distributions are roughly normal, without extreme outliers. Charles Spearman's (1904) rank-order correlation, rho, correlates ranks: it suits ordinal data, monotonic but non-linear relationships, and skewed data with outliers, since ranking tames extreme scores. Neither variable in the stem has a fixed level of measurement. Academic achievement could be percentage marks (treated as interval) or class rank (ordinal); creativity could be a score on E. Paul Torrance's Tests of Creative Thinking (1966) or a teacher's ranking of the most creative pupils. Example: if Nikhil has each student's exam percentage and Torrance score and the scatter plot looks linear, he uses Pearson's r; if the teacher has only ranked the students on creativity, he uses Spearman's rho. Option d is right because the right statistic cannot be chosen until the scale of measurement of both variables is known. Exam trap: option c sounds broad-minded, but the two coefficients are not interchangeable; Pearson's r on ranks or badly skewed data distorts the result, and Spearman's rho on good interval data throws information away.", "traps": {"a": "Pearson's r is right only if both variables are interval or ratio scores with a linear relationship and no extreme outliers; the stem does not say how either variable is measured.", "b": "Spearman's rho is right only if the data are ranks (ordinal) or are skewed or non-linear; again, the stem does not say so.", "c": "The two coefficients are not interchangeable: each fits a particular level of measurement and data shape, so 'either' is wrong; the choice depends on the measurement, which is option d."}}, {"q": "Who introduced the theory of measurement and scales of measurement in Psychology?", "concept": "The answer is a: Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) introduced the theory of measurement and the scales of measurement in psychology. Stevens, a Harvard psychophysicist, published 'On the theory of scales of measurement' in Science in 1946. He defined measurement as the assignment of numerals to objects or events according to rules, and argued that different rules create different kinds of scale. He named four levels, each adding a property: nominal (categories only, such as gender or blood group), ordinal (rank order, such as class position), interval (equal units but an arbitrary zero, such as Celsius temperature or IQ) and ratio (equal units plus a true zero, such as reaction time or weight). He also set out which statistics are permissible at each level: the mode for nominal, the median and percentiles for ordinal, the mean and standard deviation for interval, and meaningful ratios of scores only for ratio scales. The memory hook is NOIR. Stevens is also known for his power law of psychophysics (1957): perceived magnitude grows as a power of stimulus intensity. Example: Tara's survey records her classmates' religion (nominal), their rank in class (ordinal), their IQ (interval) and their monthly pocket money (ratio). Option a is right because the four-level theory of measurement scales is Stevens's best-known contribution. Exam trap: all four options are psychologists whose surnames begin with S, so the item rests on recall; Stanley (option c) is a tempting decoy because Stevens's own first name is Stanley, but Julian Stanley is known for research designs.", "traps": {"b": "Harold Schlosberg co-wrote the classic textbook Experimental Psychology with Robert Woodworth (1954) and proposed dimensions of facial expression of emotion (pleasantness-unpleasantness, attention-rejection, sleep-tension); he did not create the scales of measurement.", "c": "Julian Stanley is known for Experimental and Quasi-Experimental Designs for Research with Donald Campbell (1963), a classification of research designs and threats to validity, not of measurement scales.", "d": "B. F. Skinner founded operant conditioning and radical behaviourism (The Behavior of Organisms, 1938); he did not develop measurement theory."}}, {"q": "What sort of data is income?", "concept": "The answer is d: income is ratio data. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels: nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio. A ratio scale has all the properties of the lower levels (categories, order and equal intervals) plus a true zero, a zero that means none of the quantity. Income has every one of these. Zero rupees means no income at all; each rupee is the same amount wherever it falls on the scale; and ratios make sense, so Rs 60,000 a month is twice Rs 30,000. Height, weight, age and reaction time are other ratio variables. An interval scale also has equal units but an arbitrary zero: 0 degrees Celsius is not the absence of temperature, so 40 degrees is not twice as hot as 20. Example: Rahul earns Rs 45,000 a month and his sister Pooja Rs 90,000; saying Pooja earns twice as much is meaningful, which only a ratio scale allows. Option d is right because income has equal units and an absolute zero. Exam trap: income recorded in brackets such as low, middle and high becomes ordinal data; as a raw amount of money, though, it is ratio.", "traps": {"a": "Interval scales have equal units but an arbitrary zero (Celsius temperature, IQ scores); income has a true zero, so it goes one level higher, to ratio.", "b": "Nominal data are unordered categories (gender, religion, blood group) with no amounts; income is a quantity, not a label.", "c": "Ordinal data are ranks with unequal or unknown gaps (class position, or income brackets such as low, middle and high); raw income in rupees has equal units and a true zero."}}, {"q": "Norms is a term used in psychometrics to refer to the test performance data of:", "concept": "The answer is c: in psychometrics, norms are the test performance data of a particular group of test takers, designed to be used as a reference for comparison when evaluating or interpreting individual scores. This is the definition used by Ronald Jay Cohen and Mark Swerdlik in their textbook Psychological Testing and Assessment. A raw score on its own means little: 34 out of 50 could be excellent or poor. To interpret it, the test developer gives the test under standard conditions to a large normative (standardisation) sample, chosen to represent the people the test is meant for by age, sex, region and so on. Their scores become the norms, usually set out as percentile ranks, standard scores or age and grade equivalents, and a person's score is placed relative to this group: norm-referenced interpretation. Common types are age, grade, national, local and subgroup norms; good norms are large, representative, recent and relevant to the person tested. Example: Aditi, aged 10, scores 34 on a reading test; the manual's norms show this is at the 80th percentile for Indian 10-year-olds, so she reads better than 80 percent of her age group. Option c is right because norms exist precisely to serve as a reference group for comparison. Exam trap: norms describe how people actually perform, not how they should perform, so they are not standards; and norm-referenced interpretation (compared with other people) differs from criterion-referenced interpretation (compared with a fixed standard of mastery).", "traps": {"a": "People tested at a different time from another group describes a retest or a later cohort (as in test-retest reliability), not the reference group whose scores define norms.", "b": "A control group belongs to an experiment, as the baseline against which a treated group is compared; norms come from a standardisation sample, not from an experimental design.", "d": "A sample with no prior training in the area is simply a group of naive test takers; norms can come from any defined group, trained or not, and what makes their data norms is their use as a reference for comparison."}}, {"q": "Which of the following refers to the inter rater reliability?", "concept": "The answer is c: inter-rater reliability is the degree to which two clinicians will agree on the interpretation or scoring of a test. Reliability is consistency of measurement, and it has several forms. Inter-rater (inter-scorer or inter-observer) reliability asks whether two or more people who independently score, rate or interpret the same responses reach the same result. High agreement means the scoring is objective rather than dependent on who did it. It matters most wherever judgement enters: projective tests such as the Rorschach and the TAT, essay marking, behavioural observation and psychiatric diagnosis. Simple percentage agreement flatters raters, because some agreement happens by chance; Jacob Cohen's (1960) kappa corrects for chance agreement in categorical judgements, and the intraclass correlation is used for ratings on a scale. Example: two psychologists, Anil and Deepa, independently score the same 20 Rorschach records; if they code the same determinants for nearly every response, inter-rater reliability is high. Option c is right because agreement between independent scorers on the same test is exactly what inter-rater reliability means. Exam trap: the distractors are other psychometric terms in disguise; two tests measuring the same construct is convergent validity, predicting future behaviour is predictive validity, and items relating to each other is internal consistency.", "traps": {"a": "Two tests measuring the same construct is convergent validity, one form of construct validity, not agreement between raters.", "b": "A clinician's ability to predict future behaviour from a test is predictive validity, a form of criterion-related validity.", "d": "The degree to which the items of a test relate to each other is internal consistency reliability, usually measured with Cronbach's alpha or KR-20."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is assessed by Cronbach's alpha?", "concept": "The answer is d: Cronbach's alpha assesses internal consistency. Lee Cronbach (1951) introduced coefficient alpha to estimate how far the items of a test hang together, that is, how consistently they measure the same underlying construct. Alpha rises when the items correlate with one another and when there are more items. Conceptually it is the mean of all possible split-half coefficients, so it avoids the arbitrary choice of a single split. It generalises the KR-20 formula of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937), which works only for items scored right or wrong, to items with several score points, such as Likert ratings. About 0.70 is the usual minimum (Jum Nunnally, 1978). Example: Sneha builds a 10-item exam-anxiety scale; if students who agree with one item tend to agree with the others, alpha is high. Option d is right because alpha is the standard index of internal consistency reliability. Exam trap: the distractors are mislabelled; 'inter rater validity' and 'test retest validity' are really reliabilities (agreement between scorers and stability over time), and concurrent validity is a criterion validity checked against an outside measure taken at the same time.", "traps": {"a": "Concurrent validity is criterion-related validity: test scores are correlated with an external criterion measured at about the same time, not with the test's own items.", "b": "'Inter rater validity' is a mislabel for inter-rater reliability, agreement between independent scorers, measured with Cohen's kappa or the intraclass correlation, not with alpha.", "c": "'Test retest validity' is a mislabel for test-retest reliability, the correlation between scores from two administrations of the same test, which assesses stability over time rather than internal consistency."}}, {"q": "The process of developing a test occurs in 5 stages beginning with test conceptualization. What is the 5th stage of this process?", "concept": "The answer is b: test revision is the fifth and final stage of test development. Ronald Jay Cohen and Mark Swerdlik (Psychological Testing and Assessment, 7th edition, 2010) describe five stages. (1) Test conceptualisation: the developer decides what the test will measure, for whom and why, often after some pilot work. (2) Test construction: items are written, a scaling method is chosen (for example a Likert-type or Guttman scale) and a scoring plan is fixed. (3) Test tryout: the draft is given to a sample of people like the intended test takers, under the same conditions as the final test. (4) Item analysis: the tryout data are used to judge each item by its difficulty, its discrimination (whether high scorers pass it more often than low scorers) and, for multiple-choice items, its distractors. (5) Test revision: weak items are dropped or rewritten and the revised test is tried out again, so the last stages can loop until the test works. Example: Meera builds an exam-anxiety scale; tryout and item analysis show that six items fail to discriminate, so she rewrites them and tries the scale again. Option b is right because revision is the stage that acts on everything learned in the earlier four. Exam trap: item analysis is the fourth stage, not the fifth; and Indian texts often list longer sequences ending with norms and the manual, but five stages beginning with conceptualisation is the Cohen and Swerdlik scheme.", "traps": {"a": "Item analysis is the fourth stage: tryout data are used to work out each item's difficulty, discrimination and distractor statistics. It comes just before revision, not after it.", "c": "Test tryout is the third stage, giving the draft test to a sample like the intended test takers; its data feed item analysis, so it cannot be the last stage.", "d": "Test construction is the second stage, writing and scaling the items after conceptualisation; the process ends with revising the test in the light of item analysis."}}, {"q": "In the context of Rorschach scoring, which does not belong?", "concept": "The answer is b: 'concepts' is not a Rorschach scoring category, whereas form, determinants and popularity all belong to Rorschach scoring. Hermann Rorschach (1921, Psychodiagnostik) built a test of ten inkblots and asked people what each might be. Later scoring systems, chiefly those of Samuel Beck and Bruno Klopfer in the 1930s and John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974), which drew on them, code every response on the same main categories. Location: which part of the blot was used (W whole, D common detail, Dd unusual detail, S white space). Determinants: what feature of the blot shaped the percept: form (F), movement (M for human, FM for animal), colour (C) and shading. Content: what was seen (H human, A animal, anatomy, nature and so on). Popularity: whether the answer is a popular (P) response, given very often by the normative group, or an original one. Form quality: how well the percept fits the blot's real shape, the main index of reality testing. Example: Arjun looks at card V and says 'a bat, the whole thing, because of its shape'. That is coded W (location), F (determinant), A (content) and P (popular). Option b is right because what the person sees is coded as content; there is no category called concepts. Exam trap: 'concepts' is a near miss for 'content'; and 'form' can look like the odd one out because it is not a heading of its own, but form is the commonest determinant and form quality is scored in every major system.", "traps": {"a": "Form belongs: pure form (F) is the commonest determinant, and form quality, how well the percept fits the blot's actual shape, is scored in every major system as the index of reality testing.", "c": "Determinants are a core scoring category: they record which feature of the blot (form, movement, colour or shading) produced the percept.", "d": "Popularity belongs: popular (P) responses, percepts given very often by the normative group such as a bat on card V, are counted, and their opposite is the original response."}}, {"q": "In clinical practice, a TAT examiner administers:", "concept": "The answer is d: in clinical practice a TAT examiner gives as many cards as he or she judges useful; there is no fixed number. The Thematic Apperception Test was introduced by Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935) at the Harvard Psychological Clinic. The 1943 set has 31 cards: 30 pictures of people in ambiguous scenes plus one blank card (card 16), with some cards marked for boys, girls, men or women. For each card the person tells a story: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters think and feel, and how it ends. Murray read the stories for the hero's needs (such as achievement and affiliation) and the press of the environment. His manual planned 20 cards in two sessions of 10, the second set more unusual and dramatic. Clinicians rarely follow this plan. They choose a smaller set whose themes suit the referral question, often around 8 to 12 cards and sometimes fewer, and the choice differs from one examiner to the next. Example: a psychologist assessing Kabir, a teenager in conflict with his father, picks card 1 (a boy looking at a violin, which pulls for achievement themes) and cards showing an older and a younger man. Option d is right because the number and choice of cards are left to the examiner's clinical judgement. Exam trap: 20 cards is Murray's original plan and the number most students remember, so it lures recall; 10 is just one of his two sessions.", "traps": {"a": "'At least 3 cards' is not a rule of TAT administration; there is no set minimum, although a useful set of stories normally needs more than a handful of cards.", "b": "Ten cards was one of the two sessions in Henry Murray's original plan (two sessions of ten); it is not a fixed requirement of clinical practice.", "c": "Twenty cards was Henry Murray's original recommendation, given over two sessions, but clinicians select their own subset in practice, so 20 is the classic trap."}}, {"q": "In administering the Rorschach, the inquiry refers to that part of the examination in which:", "concept": "The answer is c: in the Rorschach, the inquiry is the part of the examination in which the cards are shown to the test taker for the second time. Hermann Rorschach (1921) published ten inkblots: five achromatic, two black and red, and three multicoloured. In the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) administration has two main phases. In the response phase (free association) the examiner hands over the cards one at a time in a fixed order, asks 'What might this be?', and records every response word for word without suggesting answers. In the inquiry phase, after all ten cards, the examiner goes back through each card and each response to learn where on the blot it was seen (location), what made it look that way, such as form, colour, shading or movement (determinants), and what it was (content), using non-leading questions such as 'What made it look like that?'. An optional testing-the-limits phase may follow, in which the examiner asks directly about popular responses or determinants the person never used. Option c is right because the inquiry depends on re-exposing the same cards so that each response can be scored. For example, if Riya said 'a bat' to Card I, in the inquiry the examiner hands her Card I again and asks her to show where the bat is and what made it look like one. Exam trap: option b, the first exposure, is the response phase; the inquiry always comes after it and never uses leading questions.", "traps": {"a": "Demographic and background information is collected in the interview before testing begins; it is not part of the Rorschach inquiry.", "b": "The first exposure of the cards is the response (free association) phase, in which the test taker says what each blot might be; the inquiry follows it.", "d": "The examiner answers the test taker's questions only briefly and non-directively (for instance 'It's up to you'); the inquiry is the examiner's structured questioning about location and determinants, not an open question period."}}, {"q": "The difference between objective and projective methods of assessment is that projective methods are:", "concept": "The answer is c: projective methods are indirect ways of assessing personality, whereas objective tests are direct. An objective (structured) test such as the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) asks clear questions about the person ('I often feel tense': true or false) and scores fixed answers with a scoring key. The person describes himself or herself, so the method is direct. Lawrence Frank (1939) coined the term projective methods for the reverse approach. The stimulus is ambiguous (an inkblot, a vague picture, half a sentence), instructions are minimal, almost any answer is accepted and the purpose is disguised. Because the stimulus has so little structure, whatever structure the person gives it is assumed to come from inside: the projective hypothesis. The examiner never asks about personality; it is inferred from how the person responds. The disguise is also the claimed advantage, since people cannot easily fake answers when they do not know what a 'good' answer looks like. Example: asked 'Do you worry about failing?', Riya can simply say no; asked to tell a story about a TAT card, she describes a girl who studies all night and still fails, and the examiner infers the worry. Option c is right because indirectness is the defining difference between the two approaches. Exam trap: 'All of the above' tempts because some projective tests use drawings and pictures can look culture-free, but most projective responses are verbal and the tests are strongly culture-bound.", "traps": {"a": "False: most projective methods are verbal (Rorschach responses, TAT stories, sentence completion, word association); only some, such as Karen Machover's (1949) Draw-A-Person test, rely on drawing, so being artistic is not what separates them from objective tests.", "b": "False: projective tests are culture-bound, because the pictures, the expected themes and the interpretive norms all come from a particular culture, which is why culturally adapted versions have had to be built.", "d": "All of the above fails because a and b are both false; only c, the indirect nature of projective assessment, is a true difference."}}, {"q": "In family therapy, the family therapist will meet:", "concept": "The answer is b: in family therapy the therapist usually meets all the members of the client's family together to analyse and change the way they interact. Family therapy, which took shape in the 1950s, rests on general systems theory (Ludwig von Bertalanffy, whose ideas are summed up in his book General System Theory, 1968): a family is a system whose parts affect one another, so a change in one member changes the whole. The person brought for help is the 'identified patient', the visible sign of a troubled system rather than its cause, and problems are kept going by circular patterns, not by one person's fault. To see those patterns the therapist holds conjoint sessions with the whole family in the room and watches who speaks for whom, who interrupts, who sides with whom and what happens when conflict starts. Virginia Satir (Conjoint Family Therapy, 1964) made such joint sessions central, and Salvador Minuchin (1974) asked families to act out their conflicts in the session (enactment). Example: fourteen-year-old Arjun is skipping school. In a joint session with his parents and sister, the therapist notices that his mother answers every question put to him while his father stays silent, a pattern that no single interview would reveal. Option b is right because analysing interaction among all the members together is the core of the method. Exam trap: some family therapists also see individuals (Murray Bowen, 1978, often coached one motivated member) or use role play and family sculpting, but the defining format is the whole family meeting together.", "traps": {"a": "Separate interviews collect each member's own account, which is individual assessment; family therapy's point is to watch the members interacting with one another in the same room.", "c": "Seeing the client with one relative at a time is dyadic work; it misses patterns that involve the whole system, such as coalitions and triangles among three or more members.", "d": "Having the client role-play the other family members resembles psychodrama or family sculpting, a technique Virginia Satir made popular; it is an occasional technique, not the standard format of meeting the family."}}, {"q": "The client's right to agree to participate in counseling, assessment, or other professional activities or services, after such services are explained and understood is:", "concept": "The answer is d: informed consent is the client's right to agree to, or refuse, counselling, assessment or any other professional service after it has been explained and understood. It has four parts. Disclosure: the professional explains the purpose and nature of the service, likely risks and benefits, alternatives, fees, the limits of confidentiality (for example, a duty to act if someone is at risk of serious harm) and the right to withdraw at any time. Understanding: the explanation is given in language the client can follow. Capacity: the person is able to decide; for a child, a parent or guardian gives permission and the child gives assent. Voluntariness: agreement is free of pressure. Consent is a continuing process, revisited whenever the service changes. The American Counseling Association's Code of Ethics (2014) covers it in section A.2, and the American Psychological Association's Ethics Code (2002, amended 2010 and 2016) in Standards 3.10, 9.03 (assessment) and 10.01 (therapy). Example: before Meera's first session, her counsellor explains how sessions work, the fee, what would have to be shared with others and that she may stop whenever she likes; Meera then signs. Option d is right because the stem is the textbook definition of informed consent. Exam trap: the other options are vague 'responsibility' words; professional responsibility is the practitioner's duty, not the client's right, and informed consent should not be confused with confidentiality, which protects what the client discloses.", "traps": {"a": "Professional responsibility is the practitioner's general duty to act competently and accountably (in the APA code it sits under Principle B, Fidelity and Responsibility); it is an obligation of the professional, not the client's right to agree after an explanation.", "b": "Personal responsibility means owning one's own choices and actions, a theme of some therapies (such as reality therapy), not the named ethical right of a client to agree to services.", "c": "'Nonprofessional responsibility' is not an ethical term at all; it is a filler built to look like option a."}}, {"q": "Which of the following interventions is based on the view that the childhood problems result from inappropriate family structure and organization?", "concept": "The answer is a: systemic family therapy, in its structural form, holds that children's problems come from an inappropriate family structure and organisation. Salvador Minuchin (Families and Family Therapy, 1974) described every family as having a structure: subsystems (spousal, parental, sibling), a hierarchy with the parents in charge, and boundaries that regulate closeness. Healthy boundaries are clear, firm yet permeable. Diffuse boundaries produce enmeshment (over-involvement, little autonomy); rigid boundaries produce disengagement (isolation, little support). Trouble also comes from cross-generational coalitions, such as a mother and son allied against the father. The child's symptom is read as a product of this faulty organisation, so the therapist joins the family, maps its structure, has members enact their conflicts in the session and then restructures the family by strengthening the parental subsystem and redrawing boundaries. Structural therapy is one of the systemic family therapies, which all treat the family as a system. Example: ten-year-old Kabir refuses school; the therapist sees that he sleeps in his mother's room while his father has been pushed out, and works to put both parents back in charge together. Option a is right because structure and organisation are the core ideas of the structural, systemic approach. Exam trap: parent training and functional family therapy are real family interventions, so 'All of the above' tempts, but neither blames faulty structure. Key note: functional family therapy also takes a systems view, and some texts would class it as systemic; but its focus is the function a behaviour serves, while the words 'structure and organisation' point to Minuchin's structural school under the systemic heading, and the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"b": "Parent training programmes (such as those built on Gerald Patterson's work on coercive parent and child cycles) rest on behavioural learning theory: the child's problem is learned through reinforcement, so parents are taught new ways of rewarding and limiting behaviour, not a new family structure.", "c": "Functional family therapy (James Alexander and Bruce Parsons, 1970s) blends systems and behavioural ideas for delinquent adolescents, but it asks what function a behaviour serves in family relationships, not whether the family's structure and organisation are faulty.", "d": "All of the above fails because parent training and functional family therapy do not locate the problem in faulty family structure; only the systemic, structural approach does."}}, {"q": "Which type of Psychotherapy views the process of helping others as a form of teaching?", "concept": "The answer is b: behaviour therapy views helping others as a form of teaching. It applies experimentally established principles of learning to psychological problems, building on Joseph Wolpe (1958), Hans Eysenck (1959) and the operant work of B. F. Skinner (1953). Its starting assumption is that problem behaviour is learned by the same laws as normal behaviour, so it can be unlearned and better behaviour learned in its place. Therapy is therefore new learning, and the therapist acts as a teacher or coach: defining the target behaviour, explaining the plan, teaching skills step by step (relaxation, assertiveness, social and problem-solving skills), modelling them (Albert Bandura's Principles of Behavior Modification, 1969, made modelling a core technique), having the client rehearse, giving feedback, setting homework and reinforcing progress. The aim is for clients to become their own therapists through self-management. Example: shy Riya cannot refuse her classmates' requests. The therapist models a polite refusal, Riya role-plays it, gets feedback and practises it with her roommate as homework. Option b is right because behaviour therapy openly casts the therapist as an instructor of new behaviour. Exam trap: client-centred therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) is itself a humanistic therapy, so a and d share the same non-directive stance in which the therapist facilitates growth and the client leads, the opposite of teaching; and 'psychotherapy' is the umbrella term, not a type.", "traps": {"a": "Humanistic therapies, such as Carl Rogers's person-centred therapy and Fritz Perls's Gestalt therapy, see the client as growing towards self-actualisation; the therapist facilitates awareness and growth rather than teaching new behaviour.", "c": "Psychotherapy is the umbrella term for all talking treatments, behaviour therapy included, so it does not name the particular approach that treats helping as teaching.", "d": "Client-centred therapy (Carl Rogers) is deliberately non-directive: the therapist offers empathy, genuineness and unconditional positive regard and lets the client lead, which is the opposite of a teaching role."}}, {"q": "Is it possible to skip a few steps in systematic desensitization or is it always necessary to go through each step in the hierarchy?", "concept": "The answer is b: skipping steps is not recommended, because the jump may bring more anxiety than the client can handle. Joseph Wolpe (1958) built systematic desensitisation on reciprocal inhibition: two incompatible responses, such as deep relaxation and anxiety, cannot occur together. The method has three parts. First the client learns progressive muscle relaxation, from Edmund Jacobson (1929; revised 1938). Next client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy of about 10 to 20 situations, each rated on Wolpe's Subjective Units of Disturbance Scale (SUDS, also read as subjective units of distress) from 0 (calm) to 100 (worst imaginable). Then, fully relaxed, the client imagines the lowest item. If anxiety appears, the client raises a finger, stops imagining and relaxes again; the item is repeated until it no longer causes anxiety, and only then does the client move up one step. Each step is small so that relaxation can outweigh the anxiety it brings. Skipping steps makes the gap too big: anxiety overwhelms the relaxation, the scene is paired with fear again, and the fear may grow stronger or the client drop out. If a gap proves too large, the therapist adds an in-between item instead of leaping. Example: Arjun, afraid of dogs, climbs from a photo of a puppy to a video of dogs, then a dog barking nearby. Option b is right because graded, step-by-step progress is the heart of the method. Exam trap: d sounds sensible because early items are easy, but the easy items are where the calm response is first built, so no part of the hierarchy is skipped.", "traps": {"a": "Rapid progress means the client moves up quickly, one item at a time, as each item stops raising anxiety; it does not justify jumping over items, because the next unpractised step may still bring strong anxiety.", "c": "Avoiding boredom is not a principle of the method; Joseph Wolpe's procedure depends on small graded steps, and skipping them to save time risks exactly the overwhelming anxiety the method is built to avoid.", "d": "Early items are the foundation: mastering them builds the relaxed response and the confidence that carry over to harder items, so no part of the hierarchy, early or late, is meant to be skipped."}}, {"q": "Career counseling evolved as a result of:", "concept": "The answer is d: career counselling evolved from all three needs, which together make up its founding idea. Vocational guidance began in the United States in the early 1900s, when industrialisation, city growth and immigration created many new kinds of jobs and many young people with no idea how to choose among them. Frank Parsons opened the Vocation Bureau of Boston in 1908, and his book Choosing a Vocation (1909) set out three steps: a clear understanding of yourself (aptitudes, abilities, interests, resources and limits); knowledge of the requirements and conditions of success in different lines of work; and 'true reasoning' about how the two fit. This became the trait-and-factor approach. Option a is the second step, learning about the world of work and preparing to enter it. Option b is the matching of the workplace's demands with what the worker needs. Option c is the matching of an individual's skills and needs with a place in the workplace. Later theories refined these strands: John Holland (1959; 1973) matched six personality types with six work environments (RIASEC), and Donald Super (1953) treated career choice as development across the life span. Example: Meera, a commerce graduate, takes an interest inventory, reads about jobs in finance and teaching, and with her counsellor reasons out which fits her best. Option d is right because each listed need is a genuine part of how career counselling evolved. Exam trap: each single option is true, so choosing any one alone gives an incomplete answer.", "traps": {"a": "True but incomplete: understanding the world of work and preparing people to enter it is Frank Parsons's second step, but the matching ideas in b and c were equally part of how career counselling arose.", "b": "True but incomplete: matching the demands of the job with the worker's needs is the person and environment fit idea later formalised by John Holland, but on its own it leaves out knowledge of the world of work and preparation to enter it.", "c": "True but incomplete: matching an individual's skills and needs with the workplace reflects Parsons's self-knowledge plus true reasoning, but alone it omits the other two strands, so the full answer is d."}}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques is a client centered therapist most likely to use?", "concept": "The answer is c: a client-centred therapist is most likely to use reflection. Carl Rogers (1942, 1951) built client-centred, later person-centred, therapy on the belief that every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive to grow, and that distress arises when the self-concept clashes with experience (incongruence). The therapist therefore does not diagnose, interpret or instruct. Instead, Rogers (1957) listed six necessary and sufficient conditions for change (including psychological contact and a client in incongruence); the therapist's three, the core conditions that let clients heal themselves, are: empathy (sensing the client's inner world as if it were one's own), unconditional positive regard (accepting the client without judgement) and congruence or genuineness (being real, not hiding behind a professional role). Reflection is the technique that shows empathy in action. The therapist restates the feeling behind the client's words, so the client feels understood and explores further. Example: Kabir says, 'My father keeps comparing me with my cousin.' The therapist replies, 'You sound hurt, and maybe tired of never being enough for him.' No advice, no theory; the direction stays with Kabir. This is why Rogers first called the approach non-directive. Option c is right because reflection of feeling is the signature, non-directive skill of the client-centred method. Exam trap: examiners surround reflection with directive look-alikes. Analysis and interpretation belong to Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, giving advice to directive counselling, and aversive conditioning to behaviour therapy; only reflection keeps the locus of evaluation inside the client.", "traps": {"a": "Analysis, the interpretation of free associations, dreams, resistance and transference, is the core of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (1900 onwards), in which the expert explains hidden meanings; Carl Rogers rejected the therapist as interpreter.", "b": "Giving advice is directive counselling, as in Edmund Williamson's trait-and-factor approach (1939), which puts the counsellor's judgement in charge; Carl Rogers held that advice undermines the client's own capacity to find answers.", "d": "Aversive conditioning pairs an unwanted behaviour with an unpleasant stimulus (for example, disulfiram for drinking) and belongs to behaviour therapy; its controlling, technique-led stance is the opposite of Carl Rogers's accepting relationship."}}, {"q": "Counselling is a profession that aims to:", "concept": "The answer is a: counselling aims to promote personal growth and productivity. Counselling is a professional helping relationship, usually with fairly well-functioning people facing developmental, educational, career, relationship or situational problems. It works mainly in the present, is often brief, and aims at growth, wellness, prevention and effective functioning, rather than at treating severe disorder, which is the main business of psychotherapy and psychiatry. The American Counseling Association's consensus definition (2010) reads: 'Counseling is a professional relationship that empowers diverse individuals, families, and groups to accomplish mental health, wellness, education, and career goals.' Goals commonly listed in textbooks are helping clients change behaviour, cope better, make decisions, improve relationships and develop their potential. Carl Rogers (1942) used the words counselling and psychotherapy almost interchangeably, but most modern texts keep counselling for the growth end of the range. Example: Kabir, a final-year student, feels stuck and anxious about his future. His counsellor helps him see his strengths, set goals and plan his study, so that he works better and feels more in charge; no diagnosis or medicine is involved. Option a is right because growth and effective, productive living are what counselling sets out to achieve. Exam trap: absolute words such as 'solely' and 'ensure' usually mark wrong options, and diagnosis and medication belong to clinical psychology and psychiatry, not to the aims of counselling.", "traps": {"b": "Diagnosis is a step in clinical and psychiatric work, a means of planning treatment rather than the aim of counselling; the option also muddles counselling with psychotherapy.", "c": "Prescribing and monitoring medication is the job of psychiatrists and other medical doctors; counsellors cannot prescribe, and their aim is growth and better functioning, not drug management.", "d": "Counselling works with feelings, thoughts and relationships as well as behaviour, so 'solely address behavior' is false; even behavioural counsellors attend to thoughts and feelings."}}, {"q": "Sensory _____ is the tendency of the sensory receptors to respond less and less to a constant stimulus.", "concept": "The answer is b: sensory adaptation is the tendency of sensory receptors to respond less and less to a constant, unchanging stimulus. It happens at the level of the receptors: when stimulation stays the same they fire less, so the sensation fades. This is useful, because it frees attention for changes in the environment, which usually matter more than things that stay the same. You stop smelling your own house, stop feeling the watch on your wrist and find a cold pool comfortable after a few minutes. Vision seems not to adapt only because the eyes are never perfectly still: tiny involuntary movements keep shifting the image onto fresh receptors. When an image is held at the same spot on the retina with special apparatus, it fades within seconds (stabilised retinal image studies by Lorrin Riggs and colleagues, 1953, and Roy Pritchard, 1961). Example: Meera walks into her aunt's kitchen and notices the strong smell of hing; ten minutes later she cannot smell it at all, though nothing has changed. Option b is right because a fading response to a steady stimulus is the definition of adaptation. Exam trap: adaptation is not habituation, a learned decrease in the brain's response to a repeated stimulus (you can still hear the ticking clock if you attend to it); and a threshold, the minimum intensity that can be detected (measured by Gustav Fechner, 1860), is a level, not a process. Dark adaptation is a different case, a rise in sensitivity in dim light.", "traps": {"a": "A threshold is the minimum intensity a person can detect (the absolute threshold of Gustav Fechner's psychophysics) or the smallest detectable change (the difference threshold); it is a level, not a process of fading response.", "c": "Perception is the brain's organisation and interpretation of sensory input; it is a broad process, not the specific weakening of receptor response to a constant stimulus.", "d": "A spectrum is a range of energies, such as the visible spectrum of about 380 to 760 nanometres of light; it says nothing about responses declining over time."}}, {"q": "The unconscious perception of stimuli that are too weak to exceed the absolute threshold for detection is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: subliminal perception is the unconscious perception of stimuli too weak or brief to exceed the absolute threshold for detection. 'Limen' is Latin for threshold, so subliminal means below the threshold. The absolute threshold is the weakest stimulus a person detects 50 per cent of the time (Gustav Fechner, 1860). A subliminal stimulus is not consciously reported, yet it can still be processed. The best evidence comes from masked priming: Anthony Marcel (1983) flashed words so briefly, followed by a pattern mask, that people could not say what they had seen, yet an unseen word still speeded their responses to a related word. Such effects are real but small and short-lived. The famous claim of James Vicary (1957) that flashing 'Eat popcorn' and 'Drink Coca-Cola' during a film raised cinema sales was a hoax that he admitted in 1962, and there is no good evidence that subliminal advertising changes what people buy. Example: Riya sees a smiling face for a few milliseconds, instantly masked; she reports seeing nothing, but then rates an unfamiliar Chinese character a little more favourably. Option c is right because the stem defines subliminal perception exactly. Exam trap: a and b are one and the same thing, the just noticeable difference or difference threshold (Ernst Weber, 1834), which concerns noticing a change between two stimuli, not perceiving a stimulus below the absolute threshold.", "traps": {"a": "The just noticeable difference is the smallest change in a stimulus that a person can detect half the time (Ernst Weber's law); it concerns differences between stimuli, not stimuli below the absolute threshold.", "b": "The difference threshold is simply another name for the just noticeable difference, so it fails for the same reason: it is about detecting a change, not unconscious perception of very weak stimuli.", "d": "Response bias, from signal detection theory (Wilson Tanner and John Swets, 1954; book by David Green and John Swets, 1966), is a person's tendency to say 'yes' or 'no' whatever the signal, shaped by expectations and motives; it is not unconscious detection of weak stimuli."}}, {"q": "The brightness of visual sensation is determined by the:", "concept": "The answer is a: the brightness of a visual sensation is determined mainly by the intensity (amplitude) of the light wave. Light is electromagnetic energy, and the human eye responds to wavelengths of about 380 to 760 nanometres. A light wave has three physical properties, and each maps onto one psychological dimension of what we see. Intensity, the amount of energy carried (the height or amplitude of the wave), is experienced as brightness: the more intense the light, the brighter it looks. Wavelength, or its inverse, frequency, is experienced as hue, the colour itself (long waves look red, short waves violet). Purity, how narrow the mix of wavelengths is, is experienced as saturation, the vividness of the colour. Hearing follows the same pattern: amplitude gives loudness, frequency gives pitch and complexity gives timbre. The link is not one to one: brightness grows much more slowly than intensity (the power law of Stanley Smith Stevens, 1957, gives brightness an exponent of about 0.33), and the eye is more sensitive to some wavelengths than others, but intensity is the main determinant. Example: when Arjun dims his phone screen at night, the colours keep the same hue but everything looks darker, because less light energy reaches his eyes. Option a is right because intensity is the physical property that brightness tracks. Exam trap: frequency (wavelength) sets hue, not brightness, just as it sets pitch in hearing; and saturation is itself a dimension of colour experience, not its cause.", "traps": {"b": "The frequency (equivalently, wavelength) of a light wave determines hue, the colour seen, such as red at long wavelengths and violet at short ones; it parallels pitch in hearing, not brightness.", "c": "Saturation is itself a dimension of colour experience, the purity or vividness of a hue, set by how narrow the mix of wavelengths is; it does not determine brightness.", "d": "'Radiation' is a filler: light is itself electromagnetic radiation, so the word names the energy in general, not the particular property (intensity) that sets brightness."}}, {"q": "Which of the following describes the process of transduction?", "concept": "The answer is b: transduction is the conversion of physical energy into neural impulses, as when light waves are converted into nerve signals in the eye. The brain cannot use light, sound waves or pressure directly; it works only with neural signals. Each sense therefore has receptor cells specialised for one kind of energy, and the conversion they perform is transduction, the first step of sensation. In the retina, rods and cones contain light-sensitive photopigments; when the rod pigment rhodopsin absorbs light it changes shape and starts a chemical chain that alters the cell's electrical activity, which other retinal cells pass on to the optic nerve. In the cochlea, sound vibrations bend hair cells; in the nose and tongue, chemical receptors respond to molecules; in the skin, mechanical receptors respond to pressure. After transduction come transmission to the brain and then perception, the brain's organisation and interpretation of the signals. Example: as Meera looks at a red rose, light reflected from the petals strikes her cones, which turn it into impulses that her brain then reads as a red rose. Option b is right because it describes exactly this energy-to-impulse conversion. Exam trap: transduction is neither transmission nor perception; and a radio played below your absolute threshold (a) is a subliminal stimulus, while stimuli that are not attended to (c) are a matter of attention, as in the inattentional blindness study of Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris (1999), not of conversion.", "traps": {"a": "A radio played below your absolute threshold is a subliminal stimulus: the energy is too weak to be detected, so it illustrates thresholds, not the conversion of energy into nerve impulses.", "c": "Stimuli that are not attended to illustrate selective attention and inattentional blindness (Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris's 1999 gorilla study); transduction still occurs for them, but the brain does not select them for awareness.", "d": "None of the above fails because b is an exact description of transduction."}}, {"q": "People who think that their ethnic, national, or religious group is superior to others are called:", "concept": "The answer is c: people who think that their own ethnic, national or religious group is superior to others are called ethnocentric. William Graham Sumner (Folkways, 1906) named the in-group ('we') and the out-group ('they') and defined ethnocentrism as the view of things in which one's own group is the centre of everything and all others are scaled and rated with reference to it. The ethnocentric person takes the customs, values and beliefs of the in-group as the natural standard, judges other groups by that standard and finds them strange, inferior or wrong. Some ethnocentrism appears in almost every culture and builds group loyalty, but strong ethnocentrism feeds stereotypes, prejudice and discrimination. Theodor Adorno and colleagues (The Authoritarian Personality, 1950) measured it with an E (ethnocentrism) scale and found that it went together with their F scale for authoritarianism, which is why the two are easily confused. Example: Kabir insists that his community's food, festivals and dress are the 'proper' ones and mocks a classmate's customs as backward; he is judging others by his own group's yardstick. Option c is right because believing one's own group superior is the very definition of ethnocentrism. Exam trap: authoritarian people are often ethnocentric, so a is the near miss, but authoritarianism is a whole personality type built around obedience to authority and rigid convention, while ethnocentrism is specifically the belief in one's own group's superiority.", "traps": {"a": "Authoritarian describes the personality type of Theodor Adorno and colleagues (1950): rigid adherence to convention, submission to authority and hostility to out-groups, measured by the F scale. Authoritarians are often ethnocentric, but the label for believing one's own group superior is ethnocentrism.", "b": "Dogmatic describes Milton Rokeach's (1960) closed mind: holding beliefs rigidly and rejecting opposing ones, whatever their political content; it concerns how beliefs are held, not ranking one's own group above others.", "d": "Rigid describes general inflexibility, difficulty changing a habit, set or way of thinking; it is a cognitive style, not a belief that one's own ethnic or religious group is superior."}}, {"q": "A real danger of groupthink is that it:", "concept": "The answer is d: the real danger of groupthink is that it leads to a suspension of critical thinking, so the group makes poor decisions. Irving Janis (Victims of Groupthink, 1972; revised 1982) defined groupthink as a mode of thinking in a highly cohesive group in which the striving for unanimity overrides members' motivation to appraise alternatives realistically. Its antecedents are high cohesion, insulation from outsiders, a directive leader, high stress and no methodical procedure for weighing options. Janis listed eight symptoms: an illusion of invulnerability, collective rationalisation, belief in the group's inherent morality, stereotyped views of out-groups, direct pressure on dissenters, self-censorship, an illusion of unanimity and self-appointed mindguards who screen out unwelcome information. The result is defective decision making: few alternatives considered, risks unexamined, information ignored and no contingency plans. His classic case was President John F. Kennedy's advisers approving the Bay of Pigs invasion of Cuba (1961). Remedies include an impartial leader, a devil's advocate, outside experts and a second-chance meeting. Example: a college fest committee led by popular Arjun approves an open-air concert in the monsoon; doubters stay quiet to keep the harmony, and nobody plans for rain. Option d is right because suspended critical thinking is what turns a friendly group into a dangerous one. Exam trap: b is true, since cohesiveness is the main precondition, but it is a condition, not the danger; and c is reversed, because groupthink makes coordination smoother, not harder.", "traps": {"a": "Groupthink does not spread from group to group like an infection; contagion is an idea from crowd psychology (Gustave Le Bon's The Crowd, 1895), not part of Irving Janis's account of group decision making.", "b": "True but not the danger: high cohesiveness is the main antecedent, the condition in which groupthink arises; the danger is what groupthink does, which is to stop members thinking critically.", "c": "Groupthink does the opposite: members agree quickly and work smoothly together, so coordination is not disrupted; what suffers is the quality of the decision."}}, {"q": "The chief function of the control group in an experiment is that it:", "concept": "The answer is b: the chief function of the control group is to provide a point of reference against which the behaviour of the experimental group can be compared. In an experiment the researcher manipulates the independent variable (IV), holds other conditions constant and assigns participants to groups at random. The experimental group receives the treatment; the control group is treated in exactly the same way except that it does not receive the treatment, or receives a placebo. The control group therefore shows what would have happened anyway, through practice, maturation, the passage of time. If the two groups were equivalent at the start and differ on the dependent variable (DV) at the end, only the IV can explain the difference. This is the logic of John Stuart Mill's (1843) method of difference: when two cases differ in only one circumstance, that circumstance is the cause. Example: Meera wants to know whether a short mindfulness session lowers exam anxiety. She randomly assigns 40 students: 20 do the session and 20 listen to a neutral audio clip of the same length, and then all complete an anxiety scale. Without the control group, lower scores might simply reflect sitting quietly for a while. Option b is right because comparison with a baseline is what a control group is for. Exam trap: the control group does not eliminate extraneous variables (c); random assignment spreads them evenly across groups, and the control group lets their effects show up equally in both.", "traps": {"a": "A mathematical (functional) relationship between IV and DV comes from testing several graded levels of the IV and plotting the results; a single control group only supplies a baseline.", "c": "No group can eliminate all extraneous variables; random assignment spreads them evenly and constant conditions limit them, and the control group then shows their combined effect rather than removing them.", "d": "False: without a control group there is no baseline, so changes caused by time, practice, maturation or placebo effects could be mistaken for effects of the IV."}}]});
